const ALL_QUESTIONS = [{"c": "FE-20001", "q": "For an EMT to perform a complete assessment of breathing the EMT must assess the rate of breathing, the pattern of respirations, and which of the following?", "a": ["Labor or effort of breathing", "Patency of the airway", "Detailed lung sounds", "Nasal examination"], "e": "When assessing breathing, you need to evaluate three core components: rate, pattern, and effort because these tell you how well the respiratory system is working and if the patient is in distress. Labor or effort of breathing reveals whether the patient is struggling to move air, which indicates respiratory compromise even when rate and pattern seem normal. While airway patency is crucial, it's assessed separately before breathing evaluation. Detailed lung sounds require auscultation skills that go beyond basic breathing assessment, and nasal examination isn't part of the fundamental breathing evaluation triad that guides your treatment decisions.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Primary Assessment"}, {"c": "FE-20002", "q": "A 45-year-old patient has fallen from a ladder and is now unresponsive with snoring respirations. Which of the following techniques should the EMT use for assessing the airway?", "a": ["Jaw-thrust maneuver", "Head-tilt chin lift", "Assess lung sounds", "Apply a nonrebreather mask at 15 LPM"], "e": "When a trauma patient is unresponsive with snoring respirations, the tongue has likely fallen back and is obstructing the airway. Since this patient fell from a ladder, you must assume potential spinal injury exists. The jaw-thrust maneuver opens the airway by lifting the mandible forward without moving the cervical spine, unlike the head-tilt chin-lift which hyperextends the neck and could worsen spinal cord damage. Assessing lung sounds won't address the immediate obstruction, and applying oxygen is premature until the airway is actually open and clear.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Primary Assessment"}, {"c": "FE-20003", "q": "Which of the following is the correct method for inserting a nasal airway?", "a": ["Posteriorly with the bevel toward the septum.", "Anteriorly and rotated 180°.", "Only in the right nostril.", "Without lubrication."], "e": "When inserting a nasal airway, proper technique prevents tissue damage and ensures effective placement. The device should be inserted posteriorly with the bevel facing toward the nasal septum, which follows the natural curve of the nasal passage and minimizes trauma to the turbinates. Inserting anteriorly or rotating 180 degrees goes against anatomical structure and increases injury risk. While either nostril can be used, the right isn't mandatory. Lubrication is essential to reduce friction and patient discomfort during insertion.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Airway Management"}, {"c": "FE-20004", "q": "How deep should the rigid “Yankauer” suction tip be inserted to suction the airway of an adult patient?", "a": ["As deep as the distance from the earlobe to the corner of the mouth", "As deep as the rigid catheter can be inserted", "Only as deep as the back molars", "Depth has no factor in suctioning"], "e": "When suctioning an adult's airway with a rigid Yankauer tip, the key is preventing stimulation of the gag reflex and potential vomiting while still reaching secretions effectively. The distance from earlobe to corner of mouth provides the safe depth that reaches the oropharynx without going too deep into the hypopharynx where the gag reflex becomes problematic. Simply inserting as deep as possible risks triggering vomiting and aspiration, while limiting depth to only the back molars leaves deeper secretions untouched, and depth clearly matters for effective clearance of the airway.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Airway Management"}, {"c": "FE-20005", "q": "What percentage of oxygen is being administered to a patient using a nasal cannula?", "a": ["24 to 44 percent oxygen", "30 to 50 percent oxygen", "40 to 65 percent oxygen", "80 to 100 percent oxygen"], "e": "Nasal cannulas deliver a relatively low concentration of supplemental oxygen because they work by adding oxygen to the patient's normal breathing pattern rather than controlling the entire breathing mixture. At typical flow rates of 1-6 liters per minute, nasal cannulas provide 24 to 44 percent oxygen concentration. Higher percentages like 30-50% or 40-65% would require non-rebreather masks or other high-flow devices, while 80-100% oxygen concentrations are only achievable with bag-mask ventilation or advanced airway devices that can deliver nearly pure oxygen.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-20006", "q": "While attempting to use the Heimlich maneuver on a conscious choking patient, they become limp and unresponsive. As the EMTs guides the patient to the floor it is\nobvious that they are not breathing and pulseless. What is the next step in the care of this patient?", "a": ["Begin CPR", "Attempt ventilations", "Perform a blind finger sweep", "Deliver 5 back blows and 5 abdominal thrusts"], "e": "When a conscious choking patient becomes unresponsive and pulseless, they've transitioned from an airway emergency to cardiac arrest, making CPR the immediate priority. The combination of no pulse and no breathing indicates the heart has stopped, requiring chest compressions to circulate blood to vital organs. While ventilations might seem logical for a choking patient, they won't be effective without circulation, and blind finger sweeps can push objects deeper into the airway. Back blows and abdominal thrusts are appropriate for conscious choking patients, but once cardiac arrest occurs, standard CPR protocol takes precedence since compressions may help dislodge the obstruction while providing essential circulation.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Airway Management"}, {"c": "FE-20007", "q": "A 45-year-old patient reports severe pain radiating into the left shoulder and jaw. The patient states the pain began while working in the yard and despite 30 minutes of rest, has had no relief from the pain. Vitals are BP 136/80, P 110, R 22, Spo2 92%. Which of the following best describes the oxygen therapy used to treat this patient?", "a": ["Administer 4 liters per minute of oxygen by nasal cannula", "Begin assisted ventilation with a bag-valve mask and 15 liters per minute of oxygen", "Administer 8 to 10 liters per minute of oxygen by simple face mask", "Administer 12 to 15 liters per minute of oxygen by non-breather"], "e": "This patient presents with classic signs of acute coronary syndrome - severe chest pain radiating to the left shoulder and jaw that started with exertion and hasn't improved with rest. The SpO2 of 92% indicates mild hypoxemia requiring oxygen support. Low-flow nasal cannula at 4 LPM is appropriate because the patient is conscious, breathing adequately at 22 respirations per minute, and doesn't need aggressive oxygenation. Bag-valve mask would be inappropriate since the patient is breathing spontaneously without respiratory distress. Simple face masks and non-rebreathers deliver higher concentrations but aren't necessary given the mild hypoxemia and stable respiratory status.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Cardiovascular"}, {"c": "FE-20008", "q": "What is the most common problem the EMT may encounter when using a bag-valve-mask device?", "a": ["Maintaining an airtight seal", "Positioning the patient's head", "Selecting the right size bag-valve-mask device.", "Lack of availability of oxygen cylinders"], "e": "Effective bag-valve-mask ventilation depends on creating positive pressure to move air into the lungs, which requires a complete seal between the mask and the patient's face. Without this seal, air escapes instead of entering the lungs, making ventilation ineffective regardless of technique. While head positioning matters for airway alignment and proper bag sizing ensures adequate volume delivery, these are typically straightforward to achieve. Equipment availability is generally a logistical rather than technical challenge, making seal maintenance the primary skill-based difficulty EMTs face during BVM use.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Airway Management"}, {"c": "FE-20009", "q": "Which of the following is used to assess the adequacy of bag-valve ventilation?", "a": ["Rise and fall of the chest", "Gurgling on exhalation", "Inflation of the cheeks", "Constriction of the pupils"], "e": "When providing bag-valve ventilation, your primary goal is ensuring adequate air exchange, which you can only confirm by watching the chest rise and fall with each ventilation. This visual indicator tells you that air is actually entering the lungs and creating the pressure changes needed for gas exchange. Gurgling sounds suggest fluid obstruction rather than effective ventilation, while cheek inflation indicates air is escaping around the mask instead of reaching the lungs. Pupil changes relate to neurological status or drug effects, not ventilation adequacy, making chest movement the most reliable and immediate assessment tool for confirming your ventilations are working.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Airway Management"}, {"c": "FE-20010", "q": "A 52-year-old patient is unresponsive with no indications of trauma. Which of the following positions should this patient be placed in for transport?", "a": ["Left lateral", "Supine", "Prone", "Semi-fowlers"], "e": "When a patient is unresponsive without trauma, the primary concern is maintaining a patent airway while preventing aspiration if vomiting occurs. The left lateral recovery position allows gravity to help drain secretions and vomit away from the airway, reducing aspiration risk. Supine positioning leaves the patient vulnerable to airway obstruction from the tongue or aspiration of fluids. Prone positioning compromises breathing mechanics and makes airway management impossible, while semi-Fowler's position offers no protection against aspiration and could worsen airway compromise in an unconscious patient.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Airway Management"}, {"c": "FE-20011", "q": "For the EMT to select the proper size oropharyngeal airway. Which of the following is the correct way to measure the distance?", "a": ["Corner of the mouth and tip of the earlobe", "Nose and earlobe", "Chin and earlobe", "Chin and nostril"], "e": "Proper oropharyngeal airway sizing prevents airway obstruction and ensures the device sits correctly without pushing the tongue backward or being too short to lift it effectively. The corner of the mouth to the tip of the earlobe provides the accurate measurement because this distance corresponds to the natural curve from the lips to the back of the throat where the airway should rest. Measuring from the nose, chin, or nostril would result in an improperly sized device that could either obstruct the airway by being too long or fail to adequately displace the tongue if too short.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Airway Management"}, {"c": "FE-20012", "q": "Which of the following devices would be the most appropriate to deliver 90% oxygen to a patient?", "a": ["Non-rebreather mask", "Simple Face mask", "Nasal cannula", "Bag-valve mask with supplemental oxygen"], "e": "When a patient needs high-concentration oxygen therapy, the non-rebreather mask is your go-to device because it can deliver up to 90-95% oxygen when properly fitted with adequate flow rates. The reservoir bag stores pure oxygen between breaths, and the one-way valves prevent exhaled air from mixing back in, maintaining that high concentration. Simple face masks only reach about 40-60% oxygen due to room air mixing, while nasal cannulas max out around 44% even at high flow rates. A bag-valve mask is primarily a ventilation device for assisting or controlling breathing rather than passive oxygen delivery, making it less appropriate for a spontaneously breathing patient who simply needs high-concentration oxygen therapy.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Ventilation"}, {"c": "FE-20013", "q": "A 28-year-old patient has just been assaulted and is now unresponsive to painful stimuli only. The patient has blood around the mouth and nose and gurgles with each breath. Respirations are 20 and non-labored. Which of the following should be performed first in the care of this patient?", "a": ["Suction the pharynx using a tonsil tip", "Log rolling the patient onto their back", "Begin assisted ventilations with a bag-valve device", "Insert an oropharyngeal airway"], "e": "When a patient has blood around the mouth and nose with gurgling respirations, the airway is compromised by secretions or blood that must be cleared immediately before any other intervention. Suctioning the pharynx removes these obstructions and takes priority over positioning, airway adjuncts, or assisted ventilations. You can't effectively ventilate through blood and secretions, and inserting an airway device could push debris deeper. The gurgling sound tells you exactly what needs to happen first - clear the airway so the patient can breathe effectively.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Airway Management"}, {"c": "FE-20014", "q": "While ventilating a patient with no suspected neck trauma with a bag-valve-mask the patient begins to vomit. Which of the following should be performed first?", "a": ["Turn the patient to the side and remove the bulk of the vomit", "Deliver four full breaths to force the vomit back down", "Suction the vomit with a soft tip catheter", "Perform 5 upward abdominal thrusts"], "e": "When a patient vomits during ventilation, aspiration becomes the immediate life threat as stomach contents can block airways and cause severe lung damage. Turning the patient to their side allows gravity to help clear vomit from the mouth and prevents it from flowing into the lungs. Forcing breaths would push vomit deeper into the airway, while suctioning alone can't handle large volumes effectively. Abdominal thrusts are for choking on solid objects, not liquid vomit that needs to drain out naturally.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Airway Management"}, {"c": "FE-20015", "q": "Which of the following would indicate that oxygen therapy is benefiting an unresponsive adult patient with respirations of 18 per minute?", "a": ["Nail beds and gums change from cyanotic to pink", "The pulse rate increases", "The blood pressure increases", "Respiratory effort becomes shallow"], "e": "When assessing oxygen therapy effectiveness, we're looking for signs that tissues are receiving adequate oxygen and perfusion is improving. The most reliable indicator is when cyanotic nail beds and gums return to pink, showing that hemoglobin is now properly saturated with oxygen and circulation is delivering it effectively to peripheral tissues. An increased pulse rate typically indicates the heart is still working harder to compensate for poor oxygenation, while increased blood pressure and shallow respirations don't directly reflect improved oxygen delivery to tissues where it's needed most.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-20016", "q": "Which of the following would indicate that a patient has a partial airway obstruction of the upper airway?", "a": ["Stridor", "Wheezing", "Coughing", "Crackles"], "e": "When the upper airway becomes partially blocked, air has to squeeze through a narrowed space, creating turbulent flow that produces stridor - a harsh, high-pitched sound heard during inspiration. This distinctive noise occurs above the vocal cords in areas like the throat or larynx. While wheezing indicates lower airway problems like asthma, coughing suggests irritation or the body's attempt to clear an obstruction, and crackles point to fluid in the lungs, only stridor specifically signals that critical upper airway narrowing that can quickly become life-threatening.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Airway Management"}, {"c": "FE-20017", "q": "Which of the following signs or symptoms would indicate that the adult patient is starting to become hypoxic?", "a": ["Increased pulse rate", "Dropping blood pressure", "Inadequate elimination of carbon monoxide", "The skin and tissues become jaundiced"], "e": "When cells don't get enough oxygen, the body's first response is to pump blood faster to deliver what oxygen is available more quickly. An increased pulse rate is the earliest and most reliable sign that hypoxia is developing, as the heart compensates for decreased oxygen delivery. Blood pressure typically drops much later in severe hypoxia when the heart can no longer compensate effectively. Carbon monoxide elimination isn't related to oxygen levels, and jaundice indicates liver problems or blood cell breakdown, not oxygen deficiency.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-20018", "q": "Which of the following would indicate the patient is in respiratory failure?", "a": ["Maximal effort with minimal movement", "One to two-syllable dyspnea", "Wheezing", "Rhonchi"], "e": "Respiratory failure occurs when the respiratory system can no longer maintain adequate gas exchange, and the classic sign is when a patient shows maximal effort with minimal chest movement - this indicates the muscles are working as hard as possible but achieving little ventilation. While one to two-syllable dyspnea suggests respiratory distress, and wheezing or rhonchi indicate airway obstruction or fluid, these findings don't necessarily mean complete respiratory failure where the effort-to-result ratio becomes critically ineffective.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-20019", "q": "The EMT has been ventilating a patient using a pocket mask for several minutes when vomiting begins. After an appropriate duration of suctioning the patient continues to vomit. Which of the following is most appropriate in the care of this patient?", "a": ["Continue to suction until the airway is clear", "Begin ventilations with a bag valve mask and 100% oxygen", "Position the patient on their left side while continuing to ventilate with the pocket mask", "Deliver abdominal thrusts to the patient until the stomach is empty, and the vomiting subsides"], "e": "When a patient continues vomiting despite suctioning, the airway remains compromised and ventilation becomes ineffective or dangerous. Continuing to suction until the airway is clear is the priority because you cannot safely ventilate through vomit, which would push gastric contents deeper into the lungs causing aspiration pneumonia. Switching to a bag valve mask while vomit is present offers no advantage and may worsen aspiration. Positioning on the left side helps drainage but doesn't address the immediate need to clear the airway before ventilating. Abdominal thrusts are inappropriate for vomiting and could increase aspiration risk by forcing more stomach contents upward.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Airway Management"}, {"c": "FE-20020", "q": "To perform chest compression on an adult patient in cardiac arrest, which of the following best describes the proper hand placement?", "a": ["Use two hands placed on the lower one-third of the sternum", "Use one hand placed on the upper one-third of the sternum", "Use two hands placed on the upper one-third of the sternum", "Use one hand placed on the lower one-third of the sternum"], "e": "Effective chest compressions require optimal force transmission to compress the heart between the sternum and spine, generating adequate blood flow to vital organs. Proper hand placement on the lower one-third of the sternum positions your hands directly over the heart's ventricles while avoiding the xiphoid process below, which could cause internal injury if compressed. Using two hands provides the necessary force for adequate compression depth of at least 2 inches in adults, while single-hand compressions lack sufficient power. Placing hands on the upper sternum misses the heart's position and reduces compression effectiveness, failing to generate the pressure needed for circulation during cardiac arrest.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Cardiovascular"}, {"c": "FE-20021", "q": "Which of the following conditions is caused by a blockage of a coronary artery (the heart’s blood supply)?", "a": ["Myocardial infarction", "Angina pectoris", "Pulmonary embolism", "Congestive heart failure"], "e": "When a coronary artery becomes completely blocked, it cuts off blood flow to part of the heart muscle, causing those cells to die from lack of oxygen - this is a myocardial infarction or heart attack. While angina also involves coronary artery problems, it's typically from partial blockage or temporary spasm that reduces but doesn't completely stop blood flow. Pulmonary embolism blocks lung blood vessels, not coronary arteries, and congestive heart failure results from the heart's weakened pumping ability rather than acute arterial blockage.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Cardiovascular"}, {"c": "FE-20022", "q": "A 63-year-old patient has difficulty breathing and coughing frothy sputum. The patient has a history of heart problems but denies chest pain at this time. Lung sounds reveal crackles over all lung fields. The patient's feet, ankles, and lower legs are swollen with pitting edema. Based on signs and symptoms which of the following conditions should the EMT suspect?", "a": ["Congestive heart failure", "Acute coronary syndrome", "Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease", "Stroke"], "e": "This patient presents with classic signs of fluid overload from a failing heart that can't pump effectively, causing blood to back up into the lungs and body tissues. The combination of frothy sputum, bilateral crackles, and pitting edema in the lower extremities points directly to congestive heart failure, where the heart's reduced pumping ability leads to pulmonary congestion and peripheral fluid retention. While acute coronary syndrome could trigger heart failure, the absence of chest pain and presence of systemic edema suggests chronic heart failure rather than an acute cardiac event. COPD typically presents with wheezing and doesn't cause the peripheral edema seen here, and stroke would present with neurological deficits rather than respiratory and circulatory symptoms.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-20023", "q": "A 82-year-old patient has collapsed and is no longer breathing and has no pulse. Bystanders have started CPR. After attaching the AED to the patient’s chest, which of the following should the EMT perform next?", "a": ["Continue CPR until the AED analyzes the rhythm", "Charge the defibrillator and press the shock", "Have everyone stand clear and press analyze", "Continue performing CPR until a pulse returns then press analyze"], "e": "Minimizing interruptions in chest compressions is critical for survival. You should continue compressions while the AED charges and only stop when the device specifically instructs you to 'stand clear' for rhythm analysis. This maximizes the Chest Compression Fraction, keeping coronary perfusion pressure high for as long as possible. Stopping too early (as soon as the pads are on) causes an unnecessary drop in blood pressure before the analysis even begins.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Resuscitation"}, {"c": "FE-20024", "q": "The EMT is caring for a patient experiencing chest pain. In the examination, the EMT follows the OPQRST approach to gather the patient assessment. In this approach, which of the following questions would be an appropriate way to ask about “P”?", "a": ["Does anything make the pain better or worse?", "When did the problem start?", "Can you describe the pain?", "On a scale of 1 to 10, 10 being the worse pain you have ever experienced, how would you rate today’s pain?"], "e": "When assessing chest pain, understanding what provokes or palliates the discomfort provides crucial diagnostic clues about the underlying cause. The 'P' in OPQRST specifically refers to provocation and palliation factors, making 'Does anything make the pain better or worse?' the correct approach. This question helps differentiate between cardiac, musculoskeletal, or respiratory causes. While timing, pain description, and severity rating are all essential components of chest pain assessment, they correspond to different letters in the OPQRST mnemonic and don't address the specific provocative or palliative factors we're targeting with 'P.'", "s": "samantha", "t": "Secondary Assessment"}, {"c": "FE-20025", "q": "A 55-year-old patient reports extreme chest pain radiating into the arm and neck. The patient describes the pain as crushing and is located directly behind the sternum. The patient also complains of difficulty breathing, and moderate indigestion. On exam, the EMT finds that the skin is pale, cool, and clammy, lips and nail beds are cyanotic, and the pulse is irregular. Based on these findings the EMT should suspect that the patient is experiencing which of the following?", "a": ["Acute myocardial infarction", "Pulmonary embolism", "Hypovolemic shock", "Hiatal hernia"], "e": "This patient presents with classic signs of acute myocardial infarction: crushing substernal chest pain radiating to the arm and neck, dyspnea, diaphoresis, and cyanosis indicating poor perfusion. The irregular pulse suggests cardiac arrhythmias commonly associated with MI. While pulmonary embolism can cause chest pain and dyspnea, it typically presents with sharp, pleuritic pain rather than crushing substernal pain. Hypovolemic shock would show different hemodynamic changes without the characteristic chest pain pattern, and hiatal hernia causes epigastric discomfort that's typically related to eating or position changes, not the acute crushing pain described here.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Cardiovascular"}, {"c": "FE-20026", "q": "What is the normal pulse rate for the adult patient?", "a": ["60 to 100 beats per minute", "80 to 100 beats per minute", "90 to 110 beats per minute", "100 to 120 beats per minute"], "e": "Understanding normal vital signs is fundamental to recognizing when a patient is stable versus requiring immediate intervention. The normal adult heart rate ranges from 60 to 100 beats per minute, representing the physiologic balance between the body's oxygen demands and cardiac output capacity. Rates consistently above 100 suggest tachycardia from various causes like stress, fever, or cardiac issues, while the narrower ranges of 80-100, 90-110, or 100-120 would miss many healthy adults whose resting heart rates naturally fall in the 60s and 70s, particularly athletes or those on certain medications.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Cardiovascular"}, {"c": "FE-20027", "q": "While performing one rescuer CPR, how often should the EMT stop to reassess the patient’s pulse?", "a": ["After each set of five cycles of compressions and ventilations", "Every few minutes after your initial assessment", "At the completion of each cycle of compressions and ventilations", "Every time you get tired and need a short rest"], "e": "During cardiac arrest, continuous chest compressions are critical for maintaining blood flow to vital organs, and interrupting compressions significantly reduces coronary perfusion pressure. Current guidelines emphasize minimizing interruptions, so pulse checks should occur after every five complete cycles (approximately 2 minutes) rather than after each individual cycle, which would create too many damaging interruptions. Checking every few minutes is too vague and potentially too long, while using fatigue as a trigger ignores the patient's physiological needs and proper timing protocols.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Resuscitation"}, {"c": "FE-20028", "q": "A 42-year-old patient has “crushing” chest pain of sudden onset that has persisted for 30 minutes. Skin is pale and clammy, with a SpO2 of 92%. Which of the following should be your first step in the management of this patient?", "a": ["Initiate oxygen therapy", "Attach the AED", "Notify medical control", "Administer Nitroglycerin"], "e": "This patient presents with classic signs of acute coronary syndrome - crushing chest pain, diaphoresis, and hypoxemia (SpO2 92%). The immediate priority is addressing tissue hypoxia, as cardiac muscle is already compromised and needs optimal oxygen delivery to prevent further damage. Oxygen therapy directly supports cellular metabolism and reduces cardiac workload. While an AED is important cardiac equipment, this conscious patient isn't in arrest. Nitroglycerin could worsen hypotension in an already compromised patient, and notifying medical control delays critical interventions. The ABCs always guide initial management, making oxygen the logical first step.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Cardiovascular"}, {"c": "FE-20029", "q": "How deep should chest compressions be delivered to an adult patient in cardiac arrest?", "a": ["At least 2 inches", "At least 1 inch", "1 to 1 ½ inches", "1 ½ to 2 inches"], "e": "Effective chest compressions must generate enough pressure to squeeze blood from the heart's chambers and create circulation to vital organs, particularly the brain and heart muscle itself. Current guidelines specify compressions should be at least 2 inches deep for adults because this depth consistently produces adequate blood flow and coronary perfusion pressure. Shallower compressions of 1 inch or 1.5 inches simply don't create enough force to effectively pump blood through the circulatory system, while the range of 1.5 to 2 inches doesn't emphasize the minimum depth needed for effectiveness.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Cardiovascular"}, {"c": "FE-20030", "q": "The rescuer must be certain to assess the patient before using the AED because the AED is incapable of determining which of the following?", "a": ["Presence of a pulse", "Lethal cardiac dysrhythmias", "Battery power level", "The need to deliver a defibrillation shock"], "e": "The fundamental limitation of AEDs lies in their inability to detect mechanical cardiac activity - they can only analyze electrical rhythms through the electrodes. While an AED excels at recognizing shockable rhythms like ventricular fibrillation and can assess its own battery status and determine shock necessity, it cannot feel for a pulse. A patient could have organized electrical activity on the monitor but no actual blood circulation (pulseless electrical activity), making pulse assessment by the rescuer absolutely critical before and after any intervention. This is why proper patient assessment remains the rescuer's responsibility, not the machine's.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Resuscitation"}, {"c": "FE-20031", "q": "Which of the following is a life-threatening and sudden complication of a myocardial infarction?", "a": ["Ventricular fibrillation", "Hypertension", "Stroke", "Congestive heart failure"], "e": "When heart muscle dies during a myocardial infarction, the damaged tissue becomes electrically unstable and can trigger chaotic, uncoordinated electrical activity called ventricular fibrillation. This deadly arrhythmia causes the heart to quiver instead of pump, stopping blood flow within seconds and leading to cardiac arrest. While hypertension might worsen heart damage, stroke could occur from clots, and heart failure may develop over time, ventricular fibrillation is the immediate killer that requires instant defibrillation to restore organized rhythm and save the patient's life.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Cardiovascular"}, {"c": "FE-20032", "q": "The chest pain associated with cardiac compromise often is described as which of the following?", "a": ["Pressure", "Sharp", "Stabbing", "Cramping"], "e": "Cardiac chest pain typically results from myocardial ischemia, where heart muscle doesn't receive adequate oxygen-rich blood. Patients consistently describe this sensation as crushing pressure, heaviness, or squeezing, often comparing it to an elephant sitting on their chest or a tight band around their torso. This characteristic pressure sensation differs markedly from the sharp, stabbing pain associated with pleuritic conditions like pneumothorax or pericarditis, or the cramping discomfort seen with musculoskeletal strain. Recognizing this classic pressure-type presentation helps distinguish cardiac events from other chest pain causes during assessment.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Cardiovascular"}, {"c": "FE-20033", "q": "A 64-year-old patient has a history of angina pectoris. Which of the following medications should the EMT expect the patient to take when chest pain occurs?", "a": ["Nitroglycerin", "Albuterol", "Valium", "Epinephrine"], "e": "Angina pectoris occurs when the heart muscle doesn't get enough oxygen-rich blood, typically due to narrowed coronary arteries. Nitroglycerin is the first-line medication for angina because it dilates blood vessels, reducing the heart's workload and improving blood flow to the coronary arteries. Albuterol treats breathing problems by opening airways, while Valium is used for anxiety or seizures. Epinephrine would actually worsen angina by increasing heart rate and blood pressure, making the heart work harder when it's already struggling for oxygen.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Cardiovascular"}, {"c": "FE-20034", "q": "As the EMT is attempting to resuscitate an adult patient in cardiac arrest, there is a “No Shock Advised” message from the AED. Of the following, which should the EMT do next?", "a": ["Begin or continue CPR", "Check for a pulse", "Assess for breathing", "Reanalyze the rhythm"], "e": "When an AED delivers a 'No Shock Advised' message during cardiac arrest, it means the patient is either in asystole or pulseless electrical activity, neither of which respond to defibrillation. The absolute priority is maintaining perfusion through high-quality chest compressions, so CPR must begin or continue immediately. Checking for a pulse or assessing breathing wastes precious time when brain death begins within 4-6 minutes, and reanalyzing the rhythm is unnecessary since the AED just completed its analysis and determined no shockable rhythm exists.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Resuscitation"}, {"c": "FE-20035", "q": "A 21-year-old patient is unresponsive after a diving injury. Which of the following indicates the patient is in neurogenic shock?", "a": ["Low blood pressure, low heart rate", "High blood pressure, low heart rate", "Low blood pressure, high heart rate", "High blood pressure, high heart rate"], "e": "Neurogenic shock occurs when spinal cord injury disrupts the sympathetic nervous system's control over blood vessels and heart rate, causing massive vasodilation and loss of cardiac stimulation. This creates the classic presentation of low blood pressure with paradoxically low heart rate, unlike other shock types where the heart compensates by beating faster. The diving mechanism suggests potential cervical spine injury, and this combination of hypotension with bradycardia is pathognomonic for neurogenic shock, distinguishing it from hypovolemic or cardiogenic shock where you'd expect compensatory tachycardia, or hypertensive presentations that wouldn't indicate shock at all.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Shock"}, {"c": "FE-20036", "q": "Nitroglycerin possesses which of the following effects when administered to patients with suspected cardiac chest pain?", "a": ["Vasodilation and increased myocardial oxygen supply", "Vasodilation and decreased myocardial oxygen supply", "Vasoconstriction and increased cardiac workload", "Vasoconstriction and increased cardiac oxygen demand"], "e": "When cardiac muscle doesn't get enough oxygen, chest pain occurs because the heart muscle is essentially crying out for more blood flow. Nitroglycerin works by relaxing the smooth muscle in blood vessel walls, causing vasodilation that directly increases oxygen delivery to the heart muscle. This vasodilation reduces the heart's workload while simultaneously improving coronary blood flow, making more oxygen available to the struggling myocardium. Vasoconstriction would worsen the problem by further restricting blood flow, and any medication that decreased oxygen supply would be counterproductive when treating cardiac ischemia.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Cardiovascular"}, {"c": "FE-20037", "q": "What is the effect of aspirin on a patient experiencing acute coronary syndrome?", "a": ["Prevents further blood clot formation", "Dissolves the existing blood clot", "Eases the patient’s pain", "Dilates coronary arteries"], "e": "In acute coronary syndrome, a blood clot blocks coronary arteries, cutting off oxygen to heart muscle. Aspirin works by irreversibly blocking platelets from sticking together, which prevents the clot from growing larger and stops new clots from forming. While aspirin does provide some pain relief through its anti-inflammatory effects, this isn't its primary cardiac benefit. Aspirin doesn't dissolve existing clots like thrombolytics do, nor does it directly dilate arteries like nitroglycerin. The key mechanism is platelet inhibition, making the blood less likely to clot and reducing the risk of complete vessel occlusion.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Cardiovascular"}, {"c": "FE-20038", "q": "A patient received a fractured rib when struck by a baseball bat. Using “Mechanism of Injury” what other injuries should the EMT suspect to exist?", "a": ["Tension pneumothorax", "Cervical spine impingement", "Evisceration of the abdomen", "Head injury"], "e": "When a baseball bat strikes the chest with enough force to fracture a rib, the mechanism of injury involves significant blunt trauma to the thoracic cavity. This same force that breaks the rib can easily damage the underlying lung tissue, potentially causing a pneumothorax or even tension pneumothorax as air escapes into the pleural space. While cervical spine injuries occur from axial loading or hyperextension mechanisms, abdominal evisceration requires penetrating trauma, and head injuries would need direct cranial impact, none of these match the lateral chest trauma pattern described in this scenario.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Head, Facial, neck, and Spine Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-20039", "q": "When splinting a fracture of the lower leg, which of the following rules should be remembered?", "a": ["Immobilize the joints above and below the injury", "Stabilize the injury by applying pressure to the fracture", "Stabilize the injury by pulling traction on the bone", "Immobilize the bones above and below the injury"], "e": "When a bone breaks, the fracture site becomes unstable and any movement can cause further damage to surrounding tissues, blood vessels, and nerves. The key principle in splinting is to prevent movement at the fracture site by immobilizing the joints above and below the injury, which eliminates the muscle contractions and joint movements that could shift the broken bone fragments. Applying direct pressure to a fracture can worsen the injury by driving bone fragments deeper into tissues, while pulling traction requires specific training and equipment to avoid causing additional harm. Simply immobilizing the bones themselves without securing the adjacent joints would still allow dangerous movement through muscle contractions and joint motion.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Orthopedic Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-20040", "q": "A 50-year-old patient has 1st degree burns on the left entire arm, 2nd degree burns to the posterior head, lower back, right anterior leg, and 3rd degree burns to the chest. Using the rule of nines what is the percentage of body surface burned?", "a": ["31.50%", "40.50%", "45%", "63%"], "e": "Accurate burn percentage calculation is critical for fluid resuscitation protocols. Using the Rule of Nines, we only calculate partial (2nd) and full (3rd) thickness burns; superficial (1st degree) burns like the one on the patient's arm are excluded. Therefore, we calculate: posterior head (4.5%), lower back (9%), right anterior leg (9%), and chest (9%), totaling 31.5%. The answer of 40.5% is incorrect because it inappropriately includes the superficial burn in the total.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Soft tissue Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-20041", "q": "The EMT is caring for a patient experiencing bleeding from a severe leg laceration to the mid-thigh. A bulky dressing has been applied and direct pressure, but the bleeding continues. Which of the following should be performed next?", "a": ["Apply a tourniquet approximately six inches above the laceration", "Apply additional bulky dressing", "Apply ice packs", "Apply pressure point control to the femoral artery"], "e": "When direct pressure and bulky dressings fail to control severe extremity bleeding, the next step is tourniquet application to completely occlude arterial flow. Placing the tourniquet six inches above the wound ensures it's positioned over healthy tissue and single bone structure for maximum effectiveness. Adding more dressings won't address the underlying arterial bleeding that's overwhelming the initial intervention. Ice packs provide minimal hemostatic benefit and delay definitive control. While femoral artery pressure points can temporarily reduce flow, they're difficult to maintain effectively during transport and don't provide the complete vascular occlusion needed for severe hemorrhage control.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Bleeding"}, {"c": "FE-20042", "q": "A 22-year-old patient has been thrown from a horse landing on an asphalt driveway. There is bleeding from the nose, a large bump to the back of the head, rapid respirations, and unequal pupils. BP 150/98, P 62, and R 12. Which of the following should the EMT suspect as the cause of this patient’s condition?", "a": ["Head injury", "Chest injury", "Hypovolemic shock", "Spinal injury"], "e": "The classic triad of altered mental status, abnormal breathing patterns, and hypertension with bradycardia points directly to increased intracranial pressure from head trauma. The unequal pupils (anisocoria) and epistaxis following significant head impact are telltale signs of brain injury. While chest trauma could cause respiratory changes, it wouldn't produce the neurological findings or Cushing's triad (hypertension, bradycardia, irregular respirations). Hypovolemic shock would present with hypotension and tachycardia, opposite of what we see here. Spinal injury alone wouldn't explain the pupillary changes or bleeding from the nose, though cervical spine precautions remain essential in any fall mechanism.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Neurology"}, {"c": "FE-20043", "q": "A 16-year-old patient reports being injured when they fell from a set of metal bleachers. There is a wound to the left arm which has a large piece of skin and muscle pulled loose from the rest of the arm. What is the name used to describe this type of wound?", "a": ["Avulsion", "Amputation", "Laceration", "Abrasion"], "e": "An avulsion occurs when skin and underlying tissue are forcibly torn away from the body while remaining partially attached, creating a flap of tissue. This matches the description of skin and muscle pulled loose but not completely separated. An amputation would involve complete separation of a body part, while a laceration is a clean cut through tissue without the tearing mechanism. An abrasion involves scraping away surface layers of skin against a rough surface. The key distinguishing feature here is the forceful tearing that creates a tissue flap, which is characteristic of avulsion injuries from falls onto hard surfaces like metal bleachers.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Soft tissue Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-20044", "q": "A 23-year-old patient has a penetrating wound to the chest which bubbles with each exhalation. Caring for this patient should include which of the following?", "a": ["Cover the wound with an occlusive dressing taped on three sides", "Cover the wound with a dry sterile dressing", "Leave the would uncovered to allow air exchange", "Place a sandbag over the injury site and secure it in place"], "e": "This patient has an open pneumothorax (sucking chest wound) where air enters the pleural space through the chest wall defect, potentially causing lung collapse. The three-sided occlusive dressing creates a one-way valve that prevents air from entering during inspiration while allowing trapped air to escape during expiration, preventing tension pneumothorax. A completely sealed dressing could trap air and worsen the condition, while dry dressings or leaving the wound open would allow continued air entry. Sandbags provide no sealing function and could actually worsen the injury through pressure.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Soft tissue Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-20045", "q": "A 56-year-old patient has been stabbed in the abdomen during a domestic disturbance. A small loop of bowel is seen protruding from the wound. Which of the following best describes the treatment of this condition?", "a": ["Cover the wound with a moist sterile dressing and tape with a plastic wrap", "Rinse the wound with saline and leave exposed to breath", "Replace the bowel loop into the abdomen", "Cover the wound with a dry dressing"], "e": "When bowel protrudes through an abdominal wound (evisceration), the primary concern is preventing desiccation and contamination of the exposed intestine. The protruding bowel must stay moist to maintain tissue viability, which is why covering it with a moist sterile dressing and sealing with plastic wrap creates the ideal environment. Rinsing and leaving exposed would cause dangerous drying, while attempting to replace the bowel risks further injury and introduces contamination. Dry dressings would also lead to tissue desiccation and potential adherence to the bowel surface.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Abdominal and Genitourinary Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-20046", "q": "A 33-year-old patient has been involved in a fall from a second-story balcony. The patient is unresponsive with slight difficulty breathing and an increased depth of respiration. As the patient coughs a slight amount of blood-tinged sputum appears. Which of the following should the EMT suspect as the cause of this patient’s injury?", "a": ["Chest injury", "Spinal injury", "Hypovolemic shock", "Head injury"], "e": "The combination of blood-tinged sputum and altered breathing pattern strongly indicates chest trauma with likely pulmonary contusion or pneumothorax from the fall. While spinal injury could cause respiratory changes, it wouldn't produce hemoptysis. Head injury might explain altered consciousness but not the bloody sputum. Hypovolemic shock could develop from chest injuries but the hemoptysis points directly to lung damage as the primary concern requiring immediate airway and breathing management.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Pathophysiology"}, {"c": "FE-20047", "q": "A 24-year-old patient reports being burned when they removed the radiator cap from an overheating car. The patient has 2nd degree burns to their head, upper front torso, and both arms. What percent of the patient's body has been burned?", "a": ["36%", "27%", "45%", "54%"], "e": "Calculating burn percentage accurately is crucial for fluid resuscitation and triage decisions. Using the Rule of Nines, the head represents 9% (0.09), upper front torso is 9% (0.09), and each arm is 9% (0.09 each). Adding these areas gives us 9% + 9% + 9% + 9% = 36% or 0.36 of total body surface area. The other percentages would either underestimate the burn severity at 27% or overestimate it at 45% and 54%, potentially leading to inappropriate fluid management. This systematic approach ensures consistent burn assessment across different providers and helps guide treatment protocols.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Soft tissue Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-20048", "q": "Which of the following best describes the care for a patient with a chemical burn from a dry powder?", "a": ["Brush the powder from the wound and rinse with copious amounts of water", "Rinse the powder from the injury site with copious amounts of water", "Brush the powder from the wound and cover it with a moist dressing", "Rinse the powder from the injury site and cover it with a moist dressing"], "e": "Chemical burns from dry powders require a two-step approach because water alone can actually activate some chemicals and worsen the burn. The priority is first removing as much dry powder as possible by brushing it away, then flushing with copious water to dilute any remaining chemical. Simply rinsing without brushing first leaves powder that can react with water, while covering with moist dressings traps chemicals against the skin and continues tissue damage. This brush-then-flush method minimizes chemical contact time and reduces burn severity.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Soft tissue Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-20049", "q": "Which of the following best describes the appropriate method of caring for a patient with a traumatic eye injury?", "a": ["Cover the injured eye with a dry dressing and bandage both eyes", "Cover the injured eye with a moist dressing and wrap it with a pressure bandage", "Cover the injured eye with a clear dressing to permit the patient to see", "Leave the injured eye uncovered and irrigate the eye with saline"], "e": "The primary goal in traumatic eye injuries is preventing further damage through eye movement, which occurs naturally when both eyes move together (called sympathetic eye movement). Covering both eyes with dry dressings eliminates this movement and protects the injured eye from contamination. Using moist dressings can promote bacterial growth, while pressure bandages risk pushing foreign objects deeper into the eye. Clear dressings don't prevent movement, and leaving the eye uncovered exposes it to debris and allows continued movement that could worsen penetrating injuries or retinal detachment.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Head, Facial, neck, and Spine Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-20050", "q": "A 28-year-old patient reports being 4 months pregnant after becoming a victim of a head-on motor vehicle collision. The patient has severe pain in the abdomen and crepitation on the back. Which of the following should be considered in the transportation of this patent?", "a": ["Transport the patient on a backboard rolled slightly to the left side", "Transport the patient without a backboard, in a semi-seated position", "Transport the patient to labor and delivery and not an emergency department", "Place the patient in the knee-chest position and transport without delay"], "e": "In pregnant trauma patients after 20 weeks gestation, the enlarged uterus can compress the inferior vena cava when supine, reducing venous return and cardiac output - a condition called supine hypotensive syndrome. At 4 months (16 weeks), this patient is approaching the critical period where left lateral positioning becomes essential. The backboard with left tilt maintains spinal immobilization while relieving vascular compression. Semi-seated positioning would compromise spinal protection given the mechanism and back crepitation suggesting possible spinal injury. The knee-chest position is inappropriate for trauma with potential spinal involvement. Emergency department transport takes priority over labor and delivery since trauma management and maternal stabilization must occur first before addressing obstetric concerns.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Obstetrics"}, {"c": "FE-20051", "q": "Which of the following would indicate that a patient has experienced a fracture to the basilar skull?", "a": ["Bruising behind the ear", "Loss of consciousness", "Oral trauma", "Swollen eyelids"], "e": "Basilar skull fractures damage the bone at the base of the skull, causing blood to leak into surrounding tissues and create distinctive bruising patterns. Bruising behind the ear, called Battle's sign, appears as the blood tracks along tissue planes from the fracture site. While loss of consciousness can occur with many head injuries, it's not specific to basilar fractures. Oral trauma and swollen eyelids might accompany severe facial injuries but don't indicate the specific location of a basilar skull fracture like the characteristic retroauricular bruising does.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Head, Facial, neck, and Spine Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-20052", "q": "A 41-year-old patient has experienced severe chest injuries requiring the EMT to apply an occlusive dressing to the right side. During transport, the EMT notices that the patient’s respirations are becoming more labored, and breath sounds are absent on the right side. Which of the following should the EMT perform first?", "a": ["Lift the three-sided dressing and allow air to escape", "Immediately begin assisting with ventilation", "Place the patient on his left side", "Place the patient on his right side"], "e": "When a patient with an occlusive chest dressing develops worsening respiratory distress and absent breath sounds on the affected side, this strongly suggests tension pneumothorax development. The three-sided dressing may have created a one-way valve effect, trapping air in the pleural space and compressing the lung and mediastinum. Lifting the dressing immediately allows trapped air to escape, relieving the pressure. While assisted ventilation might seem logical for respiratory distress, it would actually worsen a tension pneumothorax by forcing more air into the chest. Positioning changes won't address the underlying pressure problem and could delay critical decompression.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Chest Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-20053", "q": "A 35-year-old patient has been injured in a motorcycle collision when the patient was forced to straddle the gas tank at high speed. On exam, the EMT notes that both legs are opened outward, and that the pelvis is wide and flat. What is the appropriate management of this patient?", "a": ["Use a pelvic sling or folded sheet to pull the pelvis back into alignment", "Pull the legs back to the anatomical position and support by tying them together", "Perform bilateral traction splinting of the femurs", "Stabilize the legs to a backboard in the position found"], "e": "This mechanism describes an open book pelvic fracture, where the pelvis has been forcibly spread apart, disrupting the symphysis pubis and potentially the sacroiliac joints. The priority is to restore anatomical alignment and compress the pelvis to control internal bleeding from torn vessels and fractured bone surfaces. A pelvic sling or folded sheet wrapped around the greater trochanters provides the necessary circumferential compression to close the pelvis and tamponade bleeding. Simply repositioning the legs or using traction splints won't address the underlying pelvic instability, while stabilizing in the found position leaves the fracture unreduced and bleeding uncontrolled. The key is recognizing that pelvic fractures can cause life-threatening hemorrhage that requires immediate mechanical stabilization.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Trauma Overview"}, {"c": "FE-20054", "q": "A patient has received a chest wound in which blood has begun to pool inside the pleural cavity, making ventilation difficult. What is the name of this condition?", "a": ["Hemothorax", "Simple pneumothorax", "Traumatic asphyxia", "Flail chest"], "e": "When blood accumulates in the pleural space, it creates a hemothorax, which compresses the lung and impairs ventilation. The key clinical clue here is blood pooling specifically within the pleural cavity after chest trauma. A simple pneumothorax involves air rather than blood in this space, while traumatic asphyxia results from severe chest compression causing facial and upper body congestion. Flail chest describes multiple rib fractures creating paradoxical chest wall movement. Recognition of hemothorax is crucial since significant blood loss can lead to both respiratory compromise and hemorrhagic shock, requiring immediate decompression and fluid resuscitation.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Chest Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-20055", "q": "A 69-year-old male is experiencing a stroke. The patient is conscious, with a SpO2 of 93%. Which of the following best describes the treatment for this patient?", "a": ["Place the patient in a supine position and no oxygen is needed at this time", "Place the patient on 4 liters per minute of oxygen by simple face mask and place in a supine position", "Place the patient in a trendelenburg position and no oxygen is needed at this time", "Place the patient on high-flow oxygen by NRBM and place them in the left lateral position"], "e": "In stroke management, maintaining cerebral perfusion is the priority, making supine positioning ideal to optimize blood flow to the brain. With a SpO2 of 93%, this patient doesn't meet the threshold for supplemental oxygen since current guidelines recommend oxygen only when saturation drops below 92%. Trendelenburg position would be inappropriate as it increases intracranial pressure, while the left lateral position is reserved for unconscious patients to protect the airway. High-flow oxygen isn't indicated without hypoxemia and could potentially cause vasoconstriction, reducing cerebral blood flow.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Neurology"}, {"c": "FE-20056", "q": "After assuring the airway, what is the most important aspect of the management of a patient with active seizures?", "a": ["Protect the patient from injuries by applying to pad and guiding movements", "Force the teeth apart and insert a bite block", "Hold the patient as tightly as possible to control the seizure activity", "Pressing on the patient’s shoulders, attempt to reduce the strength of the seizure"], "e": "Once the airway is secured, protecting the patient from injury becomes the priority because seizures involve uncontrolled muscle contractions that can cause trauma from hitting nearby objects. Gentle padding and guiding movements away from hazards prevents fractures, lacerations, and head injuries without interfering with the seizure's natural course. Forcing objects into the mouth risks dental damage and airway obstruction, while restraining or applying pressure can cause muscle tears, fractures, or increased agitation. The goal is protection, not seizure suppression, as most seizures are self-limiting.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Primary Assessment"}, {"c": "FE-20057", "q": "Responding to a patient experiencing a seizure, the EMT arrives just in time to witness the last few convulsive movements. The patient then becomes very still and unresponsive. The patient is breathing normally and has a strong regular pulse. The EMT should consider this to be which of the following?", "a": ["Normal for seizure patients and provide supportive care during transport", "An indication of a serious injury and rapid transport is required", "A sign that the patient has experienced a stroke during the seizure", "An ominous sign and respiratory arrest are imminent"], "e": "What you're witnessing is the postictal phase, a normal and expected part of the seizure cycle where the brain recovers from the intense electrical activity. During this phase, patients are typically unresponsive, confused, or sleepy as their neurons reset and normal function gradually returns. The presence of normal breathing and a strong pulse confirms the patient is stable and not experiencing respiratory compromise or stroke-related complications. While this unresponsive state might look concerning, it's actually reassuring evidence that the seizure has ended naturally. Rushing to the hospital isn't necessary unless other complications arise, but supportive care during transport helps ensure the patient remains safe as they slowly regain consciousness over the next several minutes.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Neurology"}, {"c": "FE-20058", "q": "A 59-year-old patient has an acute onset of altered mental status. A family member tells the EMT that the patient took insulin early but has been vomiting due to an illness. The EMT should suspect which of the following conditions?", "a": ["Hypoglycemia", "Stroke", "Behavioral emergency", "Seizure"], "e": "When a diabetic patient takes insulin but then vomits and cannot keep food down, they create a dangerous mismatch between insulin action and glucose availability. The insulin continues working to lower blood sugar, but vomiting prevents glucose replacement, leading to hypoglycemia. The acute altered mental status fits perfectly with hypoglycemic encephalopathy. While stroke could cause confusion, the insulin-vomiting history makes hypoglycemia far more likely. Behavioral emergencies typically have psychiatric triggers, and seizures, though possible with severe hypoglycemia, are less common than altered mental status. Always check blood glucose early in diabetic patients with confusion.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Endocrine Disorders"}, {"c": "FE-20059", "q": "While working outside in the yard a 16-year-old patient began itching and having difficulty breathing. The patient has a rash on both arms with hives on the chest and neck. On assessment, the respirations get worse and begin significant wheezing. Which of the following best describes the treatment of this patient?", "a": ["Administer epinephrine by auto-injector and begin oxygen to maintain spo2 greater than 94%.", "Apply high-flow oxygen and begin rapid transport in a position of comfort", "Wash the patient with copious amounts of water for 15 minutes and begin rapid transport", "Place the patient supine and begin transport on high-flow oxygen"], "e": "This patient is experiencing anaphylaxis, a life-threatening allergic reaction that requires immediate epinephrine administration to counteract systemic vasodilation and bronchoconstriction. The combination of urticaria, respiratory distress with wheezing, and rapid progression indicates severe systemic involvement that only epinephrine can effectively reverse. While oxygen therapy supports oxygenation during the crisis, positioning alone or washing with water would not address the underlying immune response causing airway compromise and cardiovascular collapse. The supine position could actually worsen breathing difficulty in someone with severe bronchospasm, making the upright position of comfort more appropriate alongside definitive epinephrine treatment.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-20060", "q": "In a patient with acute abdominal pain, which of the following indicates that the patient's condition might be life-threatening?", "a": ["Signs of shock", "“Dry heave” vomiting", "A patient in severe pain lying in a fetal position", "Abdominal guarding"], "e": "When evaluating acute abdominal pain, signs of shock indicate systemic cardiovascular compromise that threatens life immediately. Shock suggests significant blood loss from internal bleeding, sepsis, or other catastrophic processes that demand urgent intervention. While dry heaving can occur with many benign conditions, severe pain causing a fetal position typically indicates localized inflammation like appendicitis, and abdominal guarding reflects peritoneal irritation, none of these directly threaten cardiovascular stability like shock does. Always prioritize airway, breathing, and circulation when triaging abdominal complaints.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Abdominal and Gastrointestinal Disorders"}, {"c": "FE-20061", "q": "On the first cool night of the season, a family of four is complaining of symptoms that range in severity from a headache, nausea, coughing, and slight difficulty breathing. All of the family have red lips and a rapid pulse. Which of the following should the EMT suspect as the cause of these patient's symptoms?", "a": ["Carbon monoxide poisoning", "Food poisoning", "Black mold poisoning", "Severe food allergy"], "e": "The combination of multiple family members experiencing headache, nausea, difficulty breathing, and tachycardia on the first cool night strongly suggests carbon monoxide poisoning from a faulty heating system. The classic cherry-red lips, though often described as a late sign, combined with the household exposure pattern makes CO poisoning the clear diagnosis. Food poisoning typically causes gastrointestinal symptoms without respiratory distress or the characteristic lip coloration. Black mold exposure usually develops gradually over time rather than acutely, and severe allergic reactions would present with hives, swelling, or anaphylactic symptoms rather than this constellation of neurological and respiratory signs affecting the entire household simultaneously.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Environmental emergencies"}, {"c": "FE-20062", "q": "Which of the following conditions is a lung disease characterized by the destruction of the alveoli, making it difficult to exchange oxygen and carbon dioxide?", "a": ["Emphysema", "Congestive heart failure", "Asthma", "Croup"], "e": "Emphysema is a chronic obstructive pulmonary disease where the alveolar walls break down and merge, creating larger but fewer air sacs that trap air and reduce surface area for gas exchange. This structural damage makes it progressively harder for patients to exhale carbon dioxide and absorb oxygen. While congestive heart failure can cause breathing difficulty due to fluid backup, asthma involves reversible airway constriction rather than permanent alveolar destruction, and croup is an upper airway infection affecting the larynx and trachea. Emphysema patients often present with barrel chest, pursed-lip breathing, and chronic shortness of breath that worsens over time.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-20063", "q": "Which of the following conditions is known for being called blue bloaters due to an obstruction in the lower airways causing hypoxemia and an hypercarbia in the\nblood?", "a": ["Chronic bronchitis", "Emphysema", "Pulmonary edema", "Epiglottis"], "e": "Chronic bronchitis creates the classic 'blue bloater' presentation because excessive mucus production in the lower airways traps carbon dioxide while blocking oxygen exchange, leading to both hypoxemia (low oxygen) and hypercapnia (high CO2). This combination causes the characteristic blue-tinged skin from poor oxygenation and fluid retention from right heart strain. Emphysema patients are typically 'pink puffers' who maintain better oxygen levels but struggle with air trapping. Pulmonary edema involves fluid in the alveoli rather than airway obstruction, while epiglottitis affects the upper airway and presents as a surgical emergency with stridor and drooling.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-20064", "q": "A 21-year-old patient has dyspnea and sharp pain in the chest. The patient has been drinking a little and had a fight with their friend and now says they are unable to stop breathing fast. BP 112/80, P 120, and R 32, rapid, and breath sounds are clear. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment for this patient?", "a": ["Tell the patient they are hyperventilating and coach them to slow their breathing", "Provide high-flow supplemental oxygen", "Have the patient breathe into a brown paper bag until they calm down", "Prepare to administer albuterol by small volume nebulizer"], "e": "This young patient presents with classic hyperventilation syndrome triggered by emotional stress and alcohol use. The clear breath sounds, normal blood pressure, and context of recent conflict point to anxiety-induced hyperventilation rather than a respiratory emergency. Coaching the patient to slow their breathing addresses the root cause by helping them regain control of their respiratory rate and break the cycle of anxiety and rapid breathing. High-flow oxygen would worsen the problem by further reducing carbon dioxide levels, while paper bag rebreathing carries risks of hypoxia and is no longer recommended. Albuterol treats bronchospasm, which isn't present given the clear lung sounds and lack of wheezing.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-20065", "q": "Why is it recommended that a patient with severe dyspnea be transported in a high-fowler position?", "a": ["Allows for more tidal volume and expansion", "Decreases cardiac output", "Takes pressure off the aorta", "It causes an increase in blood pressure"], "e": "When a patient with severe dyspnea sits upright in high-fowler position, gravity helps the diaphragm drop down and creates more space in the chest cavity for lung expansion. This positioning maximizes tidal volume by allowing the lungs to fill more completely with each breath. While positioning can affect cardiac workload and venous return, the primary benefit here is mechanical - giving the respiratory muscles and lungs optimal conditions to work. Most EMS protocols recommend this position for any patient with breathing difficulty because it immediately improves ventilation mechanics without requiring any equipment or interventions.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-20066", "q": "A 31-year-old patient is unresponsive on the bedroom floor. On examination, the EMT notes shallow respirations at 6 per minute and constricted pupils at 2 mm. These symptoms indicate that the patient has overdosed on which of the following drugs?", "a": ["Narcotics", "Barbiturates", "Tricyclic anti-depressants", "Methamphetamines"], "e": "The classic triad of respiratory depression, altered mental status, and pinpoint pupils strongly points to narcotic overdose. Opioids bind to receptors in the brain stem that control breathing and pupil size, causing the characteristic slow, shallow respirations and constricted pupils seen here. While barbiturates can cause respiratory depression and altered consciousness, they typically produce normal or dilated pupils rather than the pinpoint pupils observed. Tricyclic antidepressants usually cause dilated pupils and cardiac arrhythmias. Methamphetamines would create the opposite picture with dilated pupils, rapid breathing, and agitation. This presentation warrants immediate naloxone administration if available in your protocols.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Toxicology"}, {"c": "FE-20067", "q": "At the scene of a mass casualty incident of unknown cause at a federal building, there is a considerable number of patients that are experiencing extreme salivation, tear formation, loss of bladder control and bowel control, abdominal discomfort, and vomiting. Which of the following should the EMT consider as the cause of this situation?", "a": ["Release of a organophosphate poison", "Carbon monoxide exposure", "Food poisoning", "Sudden Acute Respiratory Syndrome"], "e": "The constellation of symptoms described here points directly to cholinergic overstimulation from organophosphate poisoning. These compounds inhibit acetylcholinesterase, causing excessive acetylcholine accumulation that produces the classic 'SLUDGE' syndrome: salivation, lacrimation, urination, defecation, gastrointestinal distress, and emesis. Carbon monoxide typically presents with headache and altered mental status without these parasympathetic signs. Food poisoning would be limited to gastrointestinal symptoms and wouldn't cause the lacrimation and excessive salivation seen here. SARS is a respiratory illness that wouldn't produce this specific symptom cluster. The federal building setting and mass casualty nature strongly suggests an intentional chemical release, making organophosphate exposure the most likely culprit requiring immediate decontamination protocols.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Mass Casualty incidents due to terrorism and disaster"}, {"c": "FE-20068", "q": "A 24-year-old patient reports extreme pain in the neck and back. The patient has a high fever and is sweating profusely. A rash is found on the patient's chest and arms. Based on the findings which of the following should the EMT consider first?", "a": ["All responders need gloves, masks, and eye protection before further patient contact", "Apply high-flow oxygen and support ventilations", "Secure the patient to a backboard and protect the cervical spine", "Wash the patient with cool water to lower the body temperature"], "e": "This constellation of symptoms - severe neck/back pain, high fever, profuse sweating, and rash - strongly suggests meningitis, a highly contagious condition that spreads through respiratory droplets. The priority is protecting all healthcare providers from exposure before any patient care begins. While oxygen support, spinal immobilization, and cooling measures may eventually be needed, they become meaningless if the entire crew becomes infected. Standard precautions with droplet protection (gloves, masks, eye protection) must be established immediately, as meningitis can be fatal to exposed healthcare workers and requires prompt antibiotic treatment if contracted.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Pathophysiology"}, {"c": "FE-20069", "q": "A 55-year-old patient has tearing pain along the spine and abdominal discomfort. On exam, the patient has weak radial pulses and a pulsating mass in his abdomen. Which of the following should the EMT suspect the patient is experiencing", "a": ["Aortic aneurysm", "Strangulating hernia", "Cholecystitis", "Kidney stone"], "e": "This patient presents with the classic triad of an abdominal aortic aneurysm: tearing back pain, a pulsating abdominal mass, and diminished peripheral pulses. The weakened radial pulses suggest compromised blood flow, while the pulsating mass indicates an enlarged aorta. Cholecystitis typically causes right upper quadrant pain without pulse changes, kidney stones produce flank pain that radiates to the groin, and strangulating hernias present with bowel obstruction symptoms rather than vascular compromise. This is a true emergency requiring immediate transport, as rupture can cause rapid exsanguination.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Cardiovascular"}, {"c": "FE-20070", "q": "Which of the following best describes the management of the hypothermic patient?", "a": ["Treat the patient gently, remove wet clothing, and rewarm with blankets", "Keep the patient cool during transport while maintaining aggressive stimulation", "Apply aggressive stimulation to keep the heart rate up and rewarm with blankets", "Begin passive rewarming"], "e": "Hypothermic patients have extremely fragile cardiac rhythms that can deteriorate into ventricular fibrillation with even minor stimulation, making gentle handling absolutely critical. The priority is preventing further heat loss by removing wet clothing and providing external rewarming with blankets while avoiding any jarring movements. Aggressive stimulation or maintaining coolness during transport both increase the risk of triggering fatal arrhythmias, while passive rewarming alone doesn't address the immediate need to stop ongoing heat loss from wet clothing.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Environmental emergencies"}, {"c": "FE-20071", "q": "Which of the following is an anatomic difference between children and adults that will affect the management of the pediatric airway?", "a": ["The infant’s head is proportionally larger than the adult’s", "The pediatric trachea is proportionally large than that of an adult", "The infant’s tongue is proportionally smaller than that of an adult’s", "The nose of the infant is proportionally larger than that of an adult’s"], "e": "The infant's proportionally larger head creates a natural flexion of the neck when lying supine, which can obstruct the airway by pushing the tongue against the posterior pharynx. This anatomical difference requires placing a towel under the shoulders to achieve proper sniffing position during airway management. While the pediatric trachea is actually narrower and more funnel-shaped than adults, and the tongue is proportionally larger (not smaller) relative to the oral cavity, these factors don't impact positioning like the oversized occiput does. The infant's nose is also proportionally smaller. Since infants instinctively breathe through their noses and likely won't open their mouths to breathe if their nose is blocked, keeping the nasal passages clear is critical.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Life span development"}, {"c": "FE-20072", "q": "Which of the following best describes oxygen delivery to the apprehensive child with respiratory distress?", "a": ["Perform blow by oxygen with the assistance of the parent.", "Place the patient on a nasal cannula", "Hold a non-rebreather directly on the patient and continue to provide calming words", "Use no device, but leave the oxygen cylinders flowing freely"], "e": "Apprehensive children in respiratory distress need oxygen delivery that minimizes fear and anxiety, which can worsen their condition. Blow by oxygen with parental assistance is ideal because parents provide comfort while oxygen flows near the child's face without direct contact. A nasal cannula may be tolerated poorly by distressed children and could increase agitation. Holding a non-rebreather mask directly on the face often terrifies young patients, potentially worsening respiratory distress despite good intentions. Free-flowing oxygen without any delivery method wastes oxygen and provides inadequate concentration. The key is balancing effective oxygen delivery with the child's psychological comfort.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-20073", "q": "The EMT just assisted in the delivery of a baby. The cord has been clamped and cut. Which of the following should be performed next in the care of this baby?", "a": ["Begin skin-to-skin contact with the mother", "Start a fundal massage", "Suction the nose and mouth.", "Aggressively pull on the placenta"], "e": "After cord clamping and cutting, the newborn's immediate priority is thermoregulation and bonding through skin-to-skin contact with the mother. This prevents heat loss, stabilizes vital signs, and promotes breastfeeding initiation. Suctioning is only needed if the airway appears obstructed, not routinely. Fundal massage addresses maternal bleeding concerns after placental delivery, while aggressive traction on the placenta can cause dangerous hemorrhage or uterine inversion. Most protocols emphasize immediate skin-to-skin contact as the next step for healthy newborns who are breathing well.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Obstetrics"}, {"c": "FE-20074", "q": "While examining a mother in labor the EMT finds a loop of umbilical cord protruding from the vaginal opening. In preparing for rapid transport, what position should the EMT place the mother in?", "a": ["In the knee-chest position", "On their left side", "Supine", "Position of comfort"], "e": "Umbilical cord prolapse creates a life-threatening emergency where the cord becomes compressed between the baby's presenting part and the mother's pelvis, cutting off oxygen to the baby. The knee-chest position uses gravity to shift the baby's weight away from the prolapsed cord, reducing compression and maintaining blood flow. While left lateral positioning helps with general circulation during pregnancy and supine positioning might seem logical for delivery, neither addresses the specific problem of cord compression. The position of comfort would be inappropriate here since this obstetric emergency requires immediate intervention to prevent fetal hypoxia, making the knee-chest position the critical first step before rapid transport.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Obstetrics"}, {"c": "FE-20075", "q": "Which of the following would be an indication of child abuse?", "a": ["Conflicting stories between caregivers and child's presentation", "Scald burns to the chest and neck", "A two-year-old who ate a bottle of aspirin", "Injuries to the hands and knees of a toddler"], "e": "Child abuse assessment requires evaluating consistency between the reported mechanism of injury and the physical findings. When caregivers provide stories that don't match the child's injuries or change their explanation, this raises significant concern for non-accidental trauma. While scald burns can occur accidentally during bathing, aspirin ingestion represents typical toddler curiosity and accessibility issues, and hand and knee injuries are expected in active toddlers learning to walk and explore. The key red flag is inconsistent histories, which often indicate attempts to conceal the true cause of injury.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Patients with Special Challenges"}, {"c": "FE-20076", "q": "Of the following, which is the most reliable sign of early hypoperfusion in a 2-year-old?", "a": ["Delayed capillary refill", "A decreased blood pressure", "Strong peripheral pulses", "Excessive tear production"], "e": "In pediatric patients, the cardiovascular system compensates remarkably well during early shock by increasing heart rate and peripheral vasoconstriction to maintain blood pressure. This means blood pressure remains normal until very late in the shock process, making it an unreliable early indicator. Delayed capillary refill greater than 2 seconds reflects this early peripheral vasoconstriction as the body shunts blood away from skin and extremities to protect vital organs. Strong peripheral pulses would actually indicate good perfusion, while excessive tearing is not associated with hypoperfusion states. Capillary refill assessment should be performed on the chest or forehead in cold environments to avoid false readings from ambient temperature effects.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Shock"}, {"c": "FE-20077", "q": "A 8-month-old child has been found unresponsive by the parents when they tried to wake the patient from their nap. The EMT assessment reveals cyanosis and rigor mortis. Which of the following should the EMT perform first?", "a": ["Concentrate on the emotional condition of the parents and provide support", "Pick up the child and perform CPR, begin immediate transport", "Notify the parents that the baby has died and begin questioning any neglect that might exist", "Notify the police of the possibility of child abuse"], "e": "When rigor mortis is present, the child has been deceased for several hours and resuscitation efforts would be futile and potentially traumatic for the family to witness. The immediate priority shifts from medical intervention to compassionate care for the grieving parents who are experiencing sudden infant death. While police notification and investigation may be necessary later, the EMT's first responsibility is providing emotional support and allowing the family to begin processing this devastating loss. Starting CPR on a child with obvious signs of death would give false hope and cause additional psychological harm to the parents during their most vulnerable moment.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Resuscitation"}, {"c": "FE-20078", "q": "The EMT is in the process of delivering a baby when the head appears. The EMT places the palm of their hand on top of the baby's head and exerts gentle pressure, controlling the delivery of the head. What should you do next?", "a": ["Check to assure that the umbilical cord is not wrapped around the baby’s neck", "Notify the police of the possibility of child abuse", "Reach into the vaginal tract and pull out the remainder of the baby’s body", "Turn the baby’s head and body so he is delivered face down"], "e": "Once the baby's head is delivered with controlled pressure to prevent rapid expulsion, the immediate priority is checking for a nuchal cord around the neck, which occurs in about 25% of deliveries and can cause serious complications if not addressed. This assessment must happen before any further delivery attempts because a tight cord can restrict blood flow or cause trauma during body delivery. Pulling the baby's body out forcefully could worsen cord compression, while turning the baby face down goes against normal delivery positioning. The controlled head delivery described is standard technique, not abusive behavior, so there's no need for police notification at this stage.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Obstetrics"}, {"c": "FE-20079", "q": "After delivering a baby, the EMT discovers that the patient is lethargic. The respirations are slow at a rate of 30 breaths per minute and heart rate of 80 beats per minute. What should the EMT do next in the care of this patient?", "a": ["Provide assisted ventilations at a rate of 40 to 60 breaths per minute", "Perform CPR", "Place the baby on low-flow oxygen with a simple face mask", "Provide deep suctioning of the mouth and nose"], "e": "A newborn with slow respirations at 30 breaths per minute and lethargy requires immediate ventilatory support, as normal newborn respiratory rates should be 40-60 breaths per minute. The heart rate of 80 indicates the baby is responding but needs aggressive respiratory assistance to prevent further deterioration. CPR isn't indicated since the heart rate is above 60, and while suctioning might be considered, the primary issue is inadequate ventilation rate. Low-flow oxygen alone won't address the bradypnea that's causing the lethargy and compromised oxygenation.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Neonatal care"}, {"c": "FE-20080", "q": "A 30-week pregnant patient has just been involved in an MVC. After conducting the scene size-up which of the following should the EMT perform next?", "a": ["Check responsiveness", "Assess airway", "Evaluate breathing", "Identify any life-threats"], "e": "Following scene size-up, checking responsiveness establishes the patient's neurological baseline and determines your assessment approach. In trauma patients, responsiveness guides whether you proceed with a conscious patient assessment or initiate immediate life-saving interventions for an unresponsive victim. While airway and breathing are critical components of the primary assessment, you must first determine if the patient can respond to verbal stimuli or requires immediate resuscitation measures. This systematic approach ensures you don't miss altered mental status from potential head injury, shock, or other complications that could affect your entire treatment plan.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Obstetrics"}, {"c": "FE-20081", "q": "Which of the following is an example of obstructive shock?", "a": ["Pericardial tamponade", "Anaphylaxis", "Sepsis", "Burns"], "e": "Obstructive shock occurs when blood flow is physically blocked, preventing adequate venous return or cardiac output. Pericardial tamponade creates this obstruction by compressing the heart within the rigid pericardial sac, limiting ventricular filling and dramatically reducing stroke volume. While anaphylaxis causes distributive shock through massive vasodilation, sepsis creates distributive shock via inflammatory mediators and capillary leak, and burns lead to hypovolemic shock from fluid loss. The key distinction is that obstructive shock involves a mechanical barrier to circulation, making Beck's triad (elevated JVD, muffled heart sounds, hypotension) a classic presentation you'll recognize in tamponade cases.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Shock"}, {"c": "FE-20082", "q": "Following the delivery of a baby and the placenta, the mother begins experiencing significant vaginal bleeding. After beginning rapid transport, which of the following should the EMT do to slow the mother’s bleeding?", "a": ["Perform fundal massage on the mother’s lower abdomen", "Pack the vagina with roller gauze", "Place the mother’s legs together and tie them closed with cravats", "Keep the baby lower than the vagina and between the mother's legs"], "e": "After delivery, the uterus must contract to compress blood vessels and stop bleeding from where the placenta was attached. Fundal massage stimulates these vital contractions by applying firm, circular pressure to the fundus (top of the uterus) through the lower abdomen. Packing the vagina can cause trauma and doesn't address the source of bleeding at the placental site. Tying the legs together restricts access for assessment and intervention without stopping uterine bleeding. Keeping the baby positioned lower than the vagina relates to cord management during delivery, not postpartum hemorrhage control.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Obstetrics"}, {"c": "FE-20083", "q": "A 2-year-old patient has received a serious injury from riding unrestrained in the backseat of a motor vehicle involved in a collision. How should this patient be transported?", "a": ["In a properly sized pediatric immobilizer", "On a long backboard with the legs slightly elevated", "Cradled in the mother's arms in the back of the ambulance", "On the stretcher in the recovery position"], "e": "Spinal immobilization is the top priority for any pediatric trauma patient, especially one involved in a motor vehicle collision where significant forces could cause cervical spine injury. A properly sized pediatric immobilizer provides age-appropriate spinal stabilization while accommodating the child's proportionally larger head and smaller body. Adult backboards can cause dangerous cervical flexion in toddlers due to their anatomy, while cradling allows movement that could worsen spinal injuries. The recovery position is only appropriate for conscious patients without trauma concerns, making it completely inappropriate here where maintaining neutral spinal alignment is critical for preventing secondary injury.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Multi-System Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-20084", "q": "A 25-year-old patient is 35 weeks pregnant with their first child. The patient reports feeling ill with a headache, seeing spots in their vision, and swollen hands and feet. BP 170/100, P 98 strong and regular, and R 20 and non-labored. Which of the following conditions should the EMT suspect?", "a": ["Preeclampsia", "Eclampsia", "Impeding birth", "HHNK"], "e": "Preeclampsia is a pregnancy-specific hypertensive disorder characterized by high blood pressure (greater than 140/90) after 20 weeks gestation, along with proteinuria or other end-organ dysfunction signs. This patient's presentation of severe headache, visual disturbances (spots), edema in hands and feet, and hypertension at 35 weeks perfectly matches preeclampsia criteria. Eclampsia would require actual seizure activity, which hasn't occurred yet. The patient shows no signs of active labor or impending delivery, and HHNK (hyperosmolar hyperglycemic nonketotic coma) is a diabetic emergency unrelated to pregnancy complications. Early recognition and rapid transport are crucial since preeclampsia can quickly progress to life-threatening eclampsia with seizures.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Obstetrics"}, {"c": "FE-20085", "q": "Which of the following best describes abandonment?", "a": ["Leaving a patient without turning the patient over to a person of equal or higher training", "Failure to locate and manage all injuries on a patient of multi-system trauma", "Leaving the scene of a hazardous environment", "A teenage mother who leaves her newborn baby on the steps of a fire station"], "e": "Abandonment in EMS occurs when you leave a patient without ensuring continuity of care through proper transfer to someone with equal or higher medical training. Once you begin patient care, you establish a duty that legally continues until you transfer that responsibility appropriately. While missing injuries during assessment represents poor clinical practice and leaving hazardous scenes may be necessary for safety, neither constitutes abandonment. The scenario about a teenage mother describes child abandonment, which is entirely different from medical abandonment. Always ensure proper handoff reports and documentation when transferring patient care to maintain the chain of medical responsibility.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Medical/Legal and Ethics"}, {"c": "FE-20086", "q": "What is the best method to protect the EMT from the spread of infectious diseases?", "a": ["Wash their hands after every patient contact", "Place masks on all patients who cough", "Wear safety glasses when caring for all patients", "Wear gloves on all patient contacts"], "e": "Hand hygiene remains the single most effective barrier against pathogen transmission because it directly removes microorganisms acquired through patient contact. While gloves provide important protection during procedures, they can develop microscopic tears and don't eliminate the need for handwashing. Masks on coughing patients help reduce droplet spread but address only respiratory pathogens, and safety glasses protect against splashes but miss the primary route of contamination through hands. The CDC emphasizes that proper handwashing after each patient encounter breaks the chain of infection most comprehensively, making it the cornerstone of infection control in EMS.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Infectious Diseases"}, {"c": "FE-20087", "q": "Which of the following should the EMT remember when dealing in an ethical manner?", "a": ["Do no harm", "Always act in a manner that serves your needs first", "Consent for treatment out ways other laws", "When in doubt, transport the patient even against his/her will"], "e": "The principle of 'do no harm' forms the foundation of all medical ethics and guides every EMT decision. This concept means we prioritize patient wellbeing above all else, ensuring our actions don't worsen their condition. Acting to serve our own needs first contradicts this fundamental duty to patients. While consent is important, it doesn't override laws like mandatory reporting requirements. Forcing transport against a competent patient's will violates their autonomy and can constitute assault, making proper documentation of refusal the appropriate response instead.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Medical/Legal and Ethics"}, {"c": "FE-20088", "q": "Acting as another individual of equal training and experiences, and under similar circumstances, would act, is defined as which of the following?", "a": ["Standard of Care", "Ethics", "Professionalism", "Duty to Act"], "e": "Standard of care represents the benchmark against which medical professionals are legally measured, defining how a reasonable practitioner with similar training would act under comparable circumstances. While ethics guides moral decision-making and professionalism encompasses behavior standards, neither establishes the specific legal framework for evaluating care quality. Duty to act refers to the legal obligation to provide care once initiated, but doesn't define performance expectations. Understanding this standard helps paramedics recognize that their actions will be judged against what their peers would reasonably do in similar situations.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Medical/Legal and Ethics"}, {"c": "FE-20089", "q": "When a person is unable to provide consent for treatment and transport, EMS providers should use which of the following to care for them?", "a": ["Implied consent", "Expressed consent", "Power of attorney", "Court order"], "e": "When patients cannot provide consent due to altered mental status, unconsciousness, or incapacity, implied consent allows EMS to provide life-saving care based on the legal assumption that any reasonable person would want emergency treatment. This differs from expressed consent, which requires the patient to actively agree to treatment. While power of attorney gives someone legal authority to make medical decisions, this document isn't typically available during emergency calls, and court orders are impractical for time-sensitive emergencies. Implied consent protects both the patient's right to emergency care and the provider's ability to act quickly when seconds matter.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Medical/Legal and Ethics"}, {"c": "FE-20090", "q": "When an EMT fails to provide care in a way that another competent EMT would care for a patent, the first EMT may be guilty of which of the following?", "a": ["Negligence", "Abandonment", "Assault", "Unprofessional activity"], "e": "Negligence occurs when a healthcare provider fails to meet the standard of care that another reasonable provider with similar training would provide under the same circumstances. This legal concept requires proving four elements: duty, breach of duty, causation, and damages. While abandonment involves leaving a patient without proper transfer of care, assault refers to threatening harm, and unprofessional activity is a broader category of misconduct. The scenario specifically describes substandard care delivery, which directly matches the definition of negligence. Understanding this standard helps EMTs recognize that they are held to the same level of competence as their peers in similar situations.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Medical/Legal and Ethics"}, {"c": "FE-20091", "q": "The EMT arrives at the scene of a traffic accident in which multiple vehicles are involved. There are two patients who appear to be unconscious. What should the EMT do as a first action?", "a": ["Request an additional ambulance", "Begin triaging the patients", "Begin immediate patient care", "Notify medical control for advice"], "e": "In multiple casualty incidents, resource management takes priority over immediate patient care because you cannot effectively treat multiple unconscious patients alone. Requesting additional resources first ensures adequate personnel arrive quickly to provide simultaneous care. While triage helps prioritize patients and immediate care addresses life threats, both require sufficient staffing to be effective with multiple critical patients. Notifying medical control can wait since standard protocols already guide initial MCI response. The key principle is matching resources to patient load before beginning treatment.", "s": "samantha", "t": "EMS Systems"}, {"c": "FE-20092", "q": "As an EMT-Basic, what is the first responsibility?", "a": ["Keep yourself as safe as possible", "Providing competent patient care", "Ensure the safety of your partner", "Transport all patients to the hospital"], "e": "Scene safety forms the foundation of all EMS operations because an injured EMT cannot help anyone and may create additional victims requiring rescue. While providing competent patient care is essential, it becomes impossible if you're hurt or incapacitated. Partner safety is important but follows personal safety in priority, and transporting every patient isn't always clinically appropriate or safe. The principle 'dead heroes save no one' guides this hierarchy - you must first ensure your own safety to effectively help others. Most EMS protocols begin with scene size-up and safety assessment for this exact reason.", "s": "samantha", "t": "EMS Systems"}, {"c": "FE-20093", "q": "A 16-year-old patient has been lethargic over the last few days. Family reports the patient has been excessively urinating, having uncontrollable thirst, and feverish. Blood glucose is 210. Based on these signs and symptoms which of the following conditions should the EMT suspect?", "a": ["New onset type 1 diabetes", "New onset type 2 diabetes", "New onset DKA", "New onset HHNK"], "e": "This classic presentation of polyuria, polydipsia, and hyperglycemia in a young patient strongly suggests new-onset Type 1 diabetes. The age factor is crucial here - Type 1 typically presents in children and adolescents when the pancreas suddenly stops producing insulin, causing these dramatic symptoms. While Type 2 diabetes can also cause high blood sugar, it usually develops gradually in older patients and rarely presents this acutely in teenagers. The blood glucose of 210 rules out hypoglycemia, and hyperkalemia wouldn't directly cause these urinary and thirst symptoms. When you see this triad of excessive urination, thirst, and high blood sugar in a young person, think Type 1 diabetes and prepare for potential diabetic ketoacidosis if the patient's condition worsens.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Pathophysiology"}, {"c": "FE-20094", "q": "Which of the following best describes EMS documentation?", "a": ["It is a legal document which describes all aspects of the patient assessment and care", "It is an ethical document which reflects the care rendered to a patient", "It should be written as briefly as possible to allow EMS personnel to return to service", "It is a record of patient care which documents fact and personal opinions"], "e": "EMS documentation serves as the primary legal record of patient care, requiring comprehensive details about assessment findings, treatments provided, and patient responses to establish a complete medical and legal timeline. While documentation should reflect ethical care standards and maintain factual accuracy, its fundamental purpose is legal protection for both providers and patients through thorough, objective reporting. Brief documentation often omits critical details that could be legally significant, and including personal opinions rather than objective observations can compromise the document's legal validity and professional credibility in court proceedings.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Documentation"}, {"c": "FE-20095", "q": "A 88-year-old patient reports a sudden headache with weakness in the left arm and muffled speech that resolved 20 minutes ago. The patient has a history of atrial fibrillation and takes blood thinners. Which of the following conditions should the EMT suspect?", "a": ["Transient ischemic attack", "Ischemic stroke", "Hemorrhagic stroke", "Embolic stroke"], "e": "When stroke-like symptoms completely resolve within minutes to hours, this points to a transient ischemic attack (TIA) rather than a completed stroke. The patient's atrial fibrillation creates a high risk for blood clots that can temporarily block brain blood flow before dissolving naturally. While ischemic and embolic strokes would cause lasting deficits, and hemorrhagic stroke typically presents with severe headache and progressive symptoms, the complete resolution of weakness and speech problems within 20 minutes is the hallmark of TIA. This patient needs immediate transport since TIAs often precede major strokes.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Neurology"}, {"c": "FE-20096", "q": "Which of the following indicates knowledge of proper body mechanics when lifting and moving a patient? ", "a": ["Keeping the weight as close to you as possible", "Maintaining a slight curvature of your back", "Using the muscles of your lower back to lift", "Twisting at the waist when moving a patient around a corner"], "e": "Proper body mechanics prioritize keeping your center of gravity stable and reducing strain on your spine during patient lifts. Keeping the weight as close to your body as possible minimizes the lever arm effect, which dramatically reduces the force your back muscles must generate to maintain control. While your spine naturally has curves, deliberately maintaining back curvature during lifting creates dangerous hyperextension. Using lower back muscles instead of your powerful leg and core muscles leads to injury, and twisting under load places rotational stress on spinal discs. The closer the patient stays to your body, the safer and more controlled your lift becomes.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Workforce Safety and Wellness"}, {"c": "FE-20097", "q": "A 5-year-old patient has been having multiple episodes of staring off but then fully recovers immediately with no lapse in memory. Which of the following conditions should the EMT suspect?", "a": ["Absence seizure", "Grand mal seizure", "Partial seizure", "Generalized seizure"], "e": "Absence seizures, formerly called petit mal seizures, are characterized by brief episodes of staring with immediate return to normal consciousness and no memory loss of the event itself. This classic presentation of brief staring spells with full recovery and intact memory perfectly matches absence seizures. Grand mal seizures involve dramatic convulsions and prolonged recovery periods, while partial seizures typically cause focal symptoms or altered awareness. Though absence seizures are technically a type of generalized seizure, the specific term 'absence seizure' is more precise for this distinctive staring pattern commonly seen in children.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Neurology"}, {"c": "FE-20098", "q": "What is the proper method of transporting an amputated finger?", "a": ["Place in an airtight bag on cool minimal ice water", "Place directly on ice", "Place in a cup of warm water", "In a biohazard bag"], "e": "The key to preserving an amputated digit is maintaining tissue viability while preventing cellular damage from temperature extremes. Placing the finger in an airtight bag on cool minimal ice water creates the ideal environment by keeping tissues moist and at the optimal temperature of around 39-50 degrees Fahrenheit. Direct ice contact causes frostbite and destroys cells needed for reattachment, while warm water accelerates bacterial growth and tissue breakdown. A biohazard bag alone provides no temperature control or moisture protection, making successful reimplantation unlikely.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Soft tissue Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-20099", "q": "A 25-year-old patient reports painful bleeding at 34 weeks pregnant. Which of the following should the EMT suspect is occurring?", "a": ["Abruptio placenta", "Placenta previa", "Spontaneous abortion", "Impending birth"], "e": "When a pregnant patient at 34 weeks presents with painful bleeding, abruptio placenta is the most likely diagnosis because the combination of pain and bleeding indicates the placenta is separating from the uterine wall prematurely. Placenta previa typically causes painless bleeding since the placenta covers the cervical opening without detaching. Spontaneous abortion occurs before 20 weeks gestation, making it impossible at 34 weeks. While impending birth could cause some discomfort, it wouldn't produce the significant bleeding described here. The pain distinguishes abruptio placenta from other causes of third-trimester bleeding, making rapid transport essential since this condition threatens both mother and baby.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Obstetrics"}, {"c": "FE-20100", "q": "A 43-year-old patient has been playing tennis on a hot summer day. The patient has a core temperature of 103* F and is no longer sweating. Which of the following treatments should the EMT perform?", "a": ["Place icepacks in the armpits, groin, and neck", "Cover in wet blankets and sheets", "Drench the patient's clothing", "Taper the air conditioner in the ambulance"], "e": "Heat stroke occurs when the body's cooling mechanisms fail, evidenced by the high core temperature and absence of sweating in this patient. The priority is rapid core cooling using the most efficient method available. Placing ice packs at pulse points like the armpits, groin, and neck targets areas where major blood vessels run close to the skin surface, allowing for maximum heat transfer from the circulating blood. While wet blankets and drenched clothing provide evaporative cooling, they work much slower than direct conductive cooling at pulse points. Simply adjusting the air conditioner provides minimal cooling effect compared to the aggressive intervention needed for heat stroke, where every minute of delayed cooling increases the risk of permanent organ damage.", "s": "samantha", "t": "Environmental emergencies"}, {"c": "FE-0001", "q": "You are called to a restaurant where a 52-year-old is choking. He is clutching his throat and attempting to cough but is making very little sound. His skin is turning pale, and his respirations are labored. What is the first action you should take?", "a": ["Perform abdominal thrusts", "Administer high-flow oxygen via non-rebreather mask", "Perform back blows followed by chest thrusts", "Encourage the patient to continue coughing"], "e": "he patient is exhibiting signs of severe airway obstruction, such as clutching his throat, pale skin, and labored breathing, which necessitates immediate intervention. Abdominal thrusts (Heimlich maneuver) are the most effective way to dislodge a foreign body airway obstruction in an adult. Encouraging the patient to continue coughing is only recommended if they are effectively coughing and their airway isn't completely obstructed. Back blows and chest thrusts are techniques used for infants and children, not for adults. Administering high-flow oxygen is important but not the immediate priority in a complete airway obstruction, as the oxygen can't reach the lungs.", "s": "working", "t": "Airway Management"}, {"c": "FE-0002", "q": "You respond to a 60-year-old patient experiencing chest pain. He is diaphoretic, has cool, clammy skin, and reports radiating pain to his jaw. His vitals are: BP 140/90, HR 88, SPO2 95 and RR 18. What action would be the next best step? ", "a": ["Assist the patient with taking 324 mg of aspirin under medical direction", "Administer nitroglycerin and monitor vitals closely", "Place the patient in a semi-Fowler’s position and initiate transport", "Provide oxygen and wait for ALS intercept"], "e": "Aspirin is a critical part of prehospital care for a patient experiencing symptoms of a myocardial infarction (heart attack). It works as an antiplatelet agent, preventing the formation of blood clots that could further block coronary blood flow. Administering 324 mg (typically four 81 mg tablets) of aspirin is one of the first steps in treatment. This intervention is safe and effective while awaiting further care and falls well within the EMT’s scope, provided medical direction approves it.", "s": "working", "t": "Cardiovascular"}, {"c": "FE-0003", "q": "Which of the following are a signs or symptom of a stroke?", "a": ["Hemiparesis", "High fever", "Jaundice", "Severe chest pain"], "e": "Hemiparesis, or weakness on one side of the body, is a classic sign of a stroke. Strokes occur when blood flow to the brain is disrupted, and identifying motor function deficits like hemiparesis helps EMTs recognize this condition early. The other options (high fever, jaundice, and severe chest pain) are associated with different medical conditions that are not directly related to stroke, such as infections, liver disease, or cardiac issues.", "s": "working", "t": "Neurology"}, {"c": "FE-0004", "q": "You are called to the scene of a motor vehicle collision. The driver, a 24-year-old , was not wearing a seatbelt and is unresponsive. There is a puddle of blood from an open fracture in her leg. What is your priority after establishing scene safety?", "a": ["Exposing the patient to assess for arterial bleeding", "Control the bleeding with direct pressure", "Stabilize the cervical spine and open the airway", "Apply a tourniquet"], "e": "In this scenario, finding and controlling arterial bleeding with direct pressure is the highest priority. The presence of a puddle of blood from an open fracture indicates severe blood loss, which is immediately life-threatening. Without blood to circulate, the patient could deteriorate quickly. While spinal stabilization and airway management are critical, managing the potential massive hemorrhage must be done first to prevent shock and ensure the patient’s survival.", "s": "working", "t": "Head, Facial, neck, and Spine Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-0005", "q": "What should the first step be when controlling bleeding?", "a": ["Apply direct pressure", "Place the patient in a position of comfort", "Apply a pressure dressing", "Apply a tourniquet if direct pressure fails"], "e": "The first step in controlling bleeding is to apply direct pressure. This basic intervention is within the EMT’s scope and is often the most effective way to stop external bleeding. Pressure helps compress the injured blood vessels, stopping or slowing blood loss. Only if direct pressure fails should an EMT escalate to using a tourniquet or other interventions like a pressure dressing.", "s": "working", "t": "Bleeding"}, {"c": "FE-0006", "q": "You arrive at a home where a 4-year-old child is experiencing difficulty breathing. He is wheezing, has nasal flaring, and is sitting in a tripod position. His mother states he has a history of asthma but hasn’t had an attack in months. What is your immediate course of action?", "a": ["Assist with the child’s prescribed inhaler", "Administer an epinephrine auto-injector", "Administer oxygen via a nasal cannula", "Transport immediately without intervention"], "e": "In this situation, because the prescribed inhaler is available, it would be a good option to use since it is indicated by the presence of wheezing. Wheezing is a sign of bronchoconstriction, common in asthma attacks, and assisting with the child’s prescribed inhaler falls well within the EMT’s scope of practice. The inhaler is specifically meant to address the child's condition, making it the appropriate intervention. Administering epinephrine would be reserved for anaphylaxis, and oxygen is supportive but does not address the underlying cause of the asthma attack.", "s": "working", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-0007", "q": "Which of the following is a normal respiratory rate for a 6-year-old child?", "a": ["18-25 breaths per minute", "25-30 breaths per minute", "12-20 breaths per minute", "30-40 breaths per minute"], "e": "For a 6-year-old child, the normal respiratory rate is 18-25 breaths per minute. Children naturally breathe faster than adults because their lungs are smaller and their bodies have higher metabolic demands. This means they need more oxygen to fuel their growth and development. Recognizing this normal rate allows EMTs to quickly identify if the child is in distress, as a faster or slower rate could indicate potential respiratory issues that need immediate attention. Different textbooks may indicate different numbers, but they will all be in near this range.", "s": "working", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-0008", "q": "You are the first responder to a multi-vehicle collision on a highway. After ensuring scene safety, you find a patient trapped in their vehicle with severe bleeding from the head and an altered mental status. What is your immediate priority?", "a": ["Establish an airway and control bleeding", "Begin extrication of the patient", "Request additional resources and wait for backup", "Immobilize the cervical spine"], "e": "In this scenario, establishing an airway and controlling bleeding is the immediate priority. The patient is trapped, has severe bleeding from the head, and an altered mental status, all of which are critical life threats. EMTs should first focus on controlling the bleeding to prevent hypovolemic shock and ensure the airway is patent to support oxygenation. While extrication and immobilization are important, they must be done after these life-threatening conditions are addressed. Airway and bleeding management come first because without circulation and oxygen, the patient will deteriorate rapidly.", "s": "working", "t": "Head, Facial, neck, and Spine Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-0009", "q": "Which of the following scenarios would require you to contact law enforcement or other agencies?", "a": ["A child with unexplained bruising on their body", "A patient who refuses transport despite a low blood sugar reading", "An elderly patient with signs of a stroke", "A patient who has been drinking but is alert and oriented"], "e": "A child with unexplained bruising on their body requires contacting law enforcement or other agencies, as this raises suspicion of child abuse. EMTs are mandated reporters in cases where abuse or neglect is suspected. Unexplained bruising, especially in unusual locations, should prompt EMTs to involve the appropriate authorities to ensure the child’s safety. The other scenarios, while important, do not require mandatory reporting unless other suspicious signs or symptoms are present.", "s": "working", "t": "Medical/Legal and Ethics"}, {"c": "FE-0010", "q": "You are called to a residence where a 3-year-old child has swallowed a small toy and is in severe respiratory distress. The child is coughing weakly and turning blue around the lips. What should you do?", "a": ["Administer back blows and chest thrusts", "Begin chest compressions", "Attempt to visualize and remove the obstruction with a finger sweep", "Encourage the child to cough and prepare to provide ventilations"], "e": "Since the child is in severe respiratory distress with a weak cough and cyanosis (turning blue), the appropriate course of action is to administer back blows and chest thrusts. This follows pediatric airway obstruction protocols, as back blows and chest thrusts can help dislodge the object causing the obstruction. Weak coughing and cyanosis indicate the child is not moving enough air, so immediate intervention is required to prevent respiratory failure. Encouraging the child to cough is only appropriate if they can still generate a strong, effective cough.", "s": "working", "t": "Airway Management"}, {"c": "FE-0011", "q": "Which of the following indicates a patient needs assisted ventilations?", "a": ["Respiratory rate of 10 with shallow breaths", "Respiratory rate of 18 and normal chest rise", "Respiratory rate of 10 with deep breaths", "Respiratory rate of 24 with accessory muscle use"], "e": "A respiratory rate of 10 with shallow breaths indicates the need for assisted ventilations. Shallow breaths suggest the patient isn't taking in enough air with each breath (inadequate tidal volume), meaning their body isn't getting enough oxygen to function properly. Although the rate of 10 is not severely low, the poor quality of breathing (shallow) is a sign that the patient needs ventilatory support to maintain adequate oxygenation. EMTs should assist the patient using a bag-valve mask or other appropriate equipment to ensure proper ventilation.", "s": "working", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-0012", "q": "You respond to a 19-year-old who was involved in a car accident. He is unconscious and presents with bruising around his eyes and blood coming from his nose. His pupils are unequal. What is your next course of action?", "a": ["Stabilize the cervical spine and prepare for rapid transport", "Insert an oropharyngeal airway and administer oxygen", "Control bleeding from the nose with direct pressure", "Apply a cervical collar and monitor his vital signs"], "e": "For this patient, with signs of a possible basilar skull fracture (bruising around the eyes, blood from the nose, and unequal pupils), the next step is to stabilize the cervical spine and prepare for rapid transport. These are classic signs of a serious head injury, and spinal immobilization is necessary to prevent any further damage to the brain or spinal cord. Rapid transport is critical to get the patient to a trauma center where they can receive definitive care, such as imaging or surgery. While bleeding control is important, maintaining spinal stability and facilitating quick transport is paramount in this case.", "s": "working", "t": "Head, Facial, neck, and Spine Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-0013", "q": "When should spinal immobilization be applied to a trauma patient?", "a": ["The patient complains of neck pain after a fall", "The patient has penetrating trauma to the chest", "The patient has altered mental status after a motorcycle accident", "The patient is intoxicated but has no signs of injury"], "e": "Spinal immobilization should be applied to a trauma patient when the patient complains of neck pain after a fall. Complaints of neck pain following trauma strongly suggest potential cervical spine injury, and immobilizing the spine is necessary to prevent any further damage. Even if other injuries are present, ensuring spinal stability is crucial when there is pain or suspicion of spinal injury. The other situations listed, like penetrating trauma or intoxication, don’t automatically require immobilization unless there are additional indications like neck pain or altered mental status.", "s": "working", "t": "Trauma Overview"}, {"c": "FE-0014", "q": "You arrive at a park where a 45-year-old woman is found incoherent. A bystander says she has a history of diabetes and was complaining of feeling weak before collapsing. Her skin is pale and clammy, and her blood sugar is 40 mg/dL. What would medical control likely advise you to do next?", "a": ["Administer oral glucose if the patient can swallow", "Begin chest compressions immediately", "Place her in a recovery position and wait for ALS", "Administer high-flow oxygen"], "e": "Since the patient’s blood sugar is 40 mg/dL, which is significantly low, the next step would be to administer oral glucose if the patient can swallow. A blood sugar level that low indicates hypoglycemia, which can cause confusion, weakness, and potentially unconsciousness. EMTs are within their scope to administer oral glucose to raise the patient's blood sugar, provided the patient is able to swallow and protect their airway. The goal is to correct the hypoglycemia before it leads to more severe complications.", "s": "working", "t": "Endocrine Disorders"}, {"c": "FE-0015", "q": "You are assisting in the delivery of a baby when the head delivers, but the shoulders become stuck in the birth canal. The baby’s face is blue and the mother is in distress. What should you do first?", "a": ["Perform a McRoberts maneuver (flexing the mother's legs toward her abdomen)", "Encourage the mother to push harder", "Apply gentle downward pressure on the baby’s head to assist delivery", "Begin chest compressions on the baby"], "e": "In the case of shoulder dystocia, the first step is to perform a McRoberts maneuver (flexing the mother's legs toward her abdomen). This position changes the angle of the pelvis and often allows the baby’s shoulders to pass through. Encouraging the mother to push harder without addressing the shoulder obstruction could cause further complications. Chest compressions on the baby would only be necessary if the baby is not breathing after delivery, and downward pressure on the head is not advised as it can increase the risk of injury.", "s": "working", "t": "Neonatal care"}, {"c": "FE-0016", "q": "What is the ideal heart rate for a newborn?", "a": ["Greater than 100 beats per minute", "Less than 100 beats per minute", "60-80 beats per minute", "180-200 beats per minute"], "e": "The ideal heart rate for a newborn is greater than 100 beats per minute. A heart rate above this threshold indicates good perfusion and oxygenation. If the newborn’s heart rate drops below 100, it is a sign that they may need assistance with breathing or resuscitation efforts. A rate below 60 would indicate the need for immediate interventions, such as chest compressions. A rate of 180-200 is abnormally high, while it is better than too low, it still needs to be noted", "s": "working", "t": "Neonatal care"}, {"c": "FE-0017", "q": "You arrive at the scene of an industrial accident where a large chemical spill has occurred. Two workers are down inside the spill zone, and there are no hazmat teams on the scene yet. What is your first priority?", "a": ["Request additional resources and secure the scene perimeter", "Enter the zone to remove the workers", "Apply personal protective equipment and provide care to the workers", "Begin triaging the patients near the spill"], "e": "In this scenario, your first priority is to request additional resources and secure the scene perimeter. Without proper protective equipment, entering the spill zone could endanger your safety and the safety of others. Securing the perimeter helps prevent further exposure to hazardous materials while waiting for a hazmat team to handle the spill. Entering the zone without protection would place you at risk of contamination.", "s": "working", "t": "Hazardous Materials"}, {"c": "FE-0018", "q": "You arrive at the scene of a 45-year-old who fell off a roof and is now unresponsive. Bystanders state he has a history of seizures. He has a large laceration on his head, is breathing rapidly, and his pulse is weak. What is the immediate course of action?", "a": ["Control the bleeding and provide cervical spine immobilization", "Place him in a recovery position and monitor for seizures", "Administer glucose for a possible seizure-related incident", "Apply a tourniquet and monitor for signs of shock"], "e": "The immediate course of action is to control the bleeding and provide cervical spine immobilization. The patient has a head injury and is unresponsive, with rapid breathing and a weak pulse, indicating possible shock or severe trauma. Controlling bleeding is critical to prevent further blood loss, and spinal immobilization is necessary due to the mechanism of injury (fall from height) to prevent spinal damage. Administering glucose would not address the critical injuries in this case.", "s": "working", "t": "Head, Facial, neck, and Spine Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-0019", "q": "You are called to a daycare where a 2-year-old boy is having difficulty breathing suddenly. His mother reports he had a \"cold\" for a few days, but now his breathing has become more labored, and he makes a high-pitched noise when inhaling. The child is sitting upright, drooling, looks anxious, and is febrile. What should you suspect?", "a": ["Croup or epiglotitits", "Foreign Body Airway Obstruction", "Impending seizures", "Diabetic emergency"], "e": "Based on the symptoms described—sudden difficulty breathing, fever, a high-pitched noise when inhaling (stridor), drooling, and anxiety—the most likely cause is epiglottitis. This condition is a severe inflammation of the epiglottis, which can obstruct the airway. Croup is also a possibility, but epiglottitis is more likely given the acute presentation. Immediate transport and keeping the child calm is critical, as any agitation could worsen the airway obstruction. Foreign body obstruction is less likely since the child had a cold for a few days.", "s": "working", "t": "Airway Management"}, {"c": "FE-0020", "q": "You arrive at a park where a 6-year-old girl is having an asthma attack. She is wheezing, struggling to breathe, and has cyanosis around her lips. Her mother says they have an inhaler but can’t find it. The child’s pulse is 120 and respirations are 30. What should you do first?", "a": ["Place the child on high-flow oxygen and prepare for transport", "Contact medical control to assist with a bronchodilator from your equipment", "Assist the child in the tripod position and encourage slow, deep breaths", "Provide back blows and abdominal thrusts"], "e": "In this situation, the appropriate first step is to place the child on high-flow oxygen and prepare for transport. Since the child’s inhaler cannot be found, high-flow oxygen is the next best option to help improve oxygenation during the asthma attack. Contacting medical control to assist with a bronchodilator is a good step, but oxygen therapy should be initiated immediately to support the child's breathing. The tripod position is helpful, but high-flow oxygen takes priority due to the child's cyanosis and respiratory distress.", "s": "working", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-0021", "q": "You are called to a home where a 4-year-old boy is choking after swallowing a small piece of food. He is coughing forcefully but appears to be struggling. What is your next course of action?", "a": ["Encourage the child to continue coughing and monitor closely", "Perform abdominal thrusts immediately", "Perform a blind finger sweep to remove the object", "Place the child in the recovery position"], "e": "Since the child is coughing forcefully, the best course of action is to encourage the child to continue coughing and monitor closely. Forceful coughing is the body's most effective way to clear an obstruction from the airway, and intervening too soon with abdominal thrusts could cause further harm. Abdominal thrusts should only be performed if the child is unable to cough or becomes unresponsive. Performing a blind finger sweep is not recommended as it can push the object further into the airway.", "s": "working", "t": "Airway Management"}, {"c": "FE-0022", "q": "You are called to a playground where a 7-year-old boy fell off a swing and hit his head. He was briefly unconscious but is now awake and crying. His vitals are stable, but he has a large bump on his head and says he feels dizzy and his neck hurts. What is your next course of action?", "a": ["Immobilize the child on a backboard and transport to the hospital", "Apply a cold pack to the bump and monitor for signs of shock", "Place the child in a recovery position and monitor for seizures", "Encourage the child to walk around to assess his mobility"], "e": "In this scenario, since the child experienced a brief loss of consciousness and is now reporting dizziness after hitting his head, the appropriate course of action is to immobilize the child on a backboard and transport to the hospital. Any loss of consciousness following a head injury raises concerns for a potential concussion or more severe traumatic brain injury. Immobilizing the child helps prevent any further injury to the spine or brain while ensuring they receive medical evaluation. Walking around could worsen any potential injury, and while a cold pack can help, it doesn’t address the possibility of internal injuries.", "s": "working", "t": "Neurology"}, {"c": "FE-0023", "q": "A 5-year-old boy accidentally spilled boiling water on his arm, causing partial-thickness burns over his forearm and hand. The skin is red, blistered, and painful. The boy is crying but alert. What is your immediate intervention?", "a": ["Cover the burn with a clean, dry dressing and transport", "Apply a burn ointment and dress the burn", "Immerse the burn in cold water for 15-20 minutes", "Apply a cold pack directly to the burn to reduce swelling"], "e": "For partial-thickness burns (second-degree) with blistering and redness, the immediate intervention is to cover the burn with a clean, dry dressing and transport. This protects the area from infection while minimizing further damage to the skin. Applying ointments, cold packs, or immersing the burn in water for prolonged periods can cause more harm and increase the risk of infection or hypothermia in a young child. Keeping the burn clean and covered is the standard care within the EMT’s scope of practice.", "s": "working", "t": "Soft tissue Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-0024", "q": "You respond to a school where an 8-year-old girl has fallen off the monkey bars and is crying in pain. Her left arm appears deformed and is tender to touch. She is conscious and stable. What should you do first?", "a": ["Splint the arm in the position found and transport", "Apply ice directly to the fracture site to reduce pain", "Apply a tourniquet to prevent swelling", "Attempt to realign the fracture to improve circulation"], "e": "In this situation, the first step is to splint the arm in the position found and transport. Any attempt to realign the fracture could cause additional injury, and applying a tourniquet is unnecessary unless there is severe uncontrolled bleeding. Splinting in place prevents further movement, reduces pain, and minimizes the risk of further injury. Ice can help with swelling, but it should not be applied directly to a fracture site. The priority is stabilization of the injury before transport.", "s": "working", "t": "Orthopedic Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-0025", "q": "You are called to a home where a 3-year-old boy had a seizure lasting about 2 minutes. He now appears postictal, with a temperature of 102°F. His mother says he had a fever earlier, but no history of seizures. What is your next course of action?", "a": ["Place the child in the recovery position, monitor airway, and transport", "Administer antipyretics and observe the child for 15 minutes", "Apply ice packs to reduce the fever and prevent another seizure", "Perform a neurological exam and check blood glucose levels"], "e": "After a seizure in a child, especially one associated with a fever, the appropriate action is to place the child in the recovery position, monitor airway, and transport. Febrile seizures are common in children with sudden spikes in temperature, but EMTs should still monitor the airway closely in the postictal phase (the period after a seizure) to ensure that the child is breathing effectively and has not aspirated. Antipyretics (like Tylenol) are outside the EMT’s scope, and cold applications may not be effective for reducing fever rapidly. A neurological exam would be important, but maintaining a patent airway and preparing for transport are the priorities.", "s": "working", "t": "Monitoring Devices"}, {"c": "FE-0026", "q": "A 6-year-old girl is at a birthday party and suddenly develops hives, difficulty breathing, and swelling of her lips after eating cake with peanuts. She has a known peanut allergy but left her epinephrine auto-injector at home. What should you do?", "a": ["Assist the child in taking her epinephrine auto-injector and transport immediately", "Apply a cold compress to the swollen areas and monitor vital signs", "Administer epinephrine from your EMS bag and wait for symptoms to subside", "Administer diphenhydramine and wait for symptoms to subside"], "e": "Since the child is having an allergic reaction (anaphylaxis) with difficulty breathing and swelling of the lips due to a known peanut allergy, and her epinephrine auto-injector is unavailable, the correct course of action is to administer epinephrine from your EMS bag and transport immediately. EMTs are trained to administer epinephrine for life-threatening allergic reactions. Waiting for symptoms to subside or using an antihistamine like diphenhydramine would delay critical treatment, and applying cold compresses wouldn’t address the airway compromise.", "s": "working", "t": "Immunology"}, {"c": "FE-0027", "q": "You are called to a campsite where a 7-year-old girl is vomiting and lethargic after playing in the sun all day. Her skin is dry, she’s weak, and her pulse is rapid and weak. What should you do first?", "a": ["Begin cooling the child and transport her to the hospital", "Administer oral rehydration salts and transport immediately", "Provide sips of water and reassess vital signs", "Give the child sugary drinks and monitor for improvement"], "e": "This child is showing signs of heat stroke, a life-threatening condition characterized by dry skin, weakness, and a rapid, weak pulse after prolonged sun exposure. The first step is to begin cooling the child and transport her to the hospital. Heat stroke requires immediate cooling measures, such as removing excess clothing, fanning, and applying cool, wet cloths. Oral rehydration or sugary drinks would not address the severity of her condition, and cooling must begin immediately to prevent further damage.", "s": "working", "t": "Environmental emergencies"}, {"c": "FE-0028", "q": "You assist in the delivery of a full-term newborn. At 1 minute post-delivery, the baby has a heart rate of 90 bpm, weak crying, some flexion of the arms and legs, grimaces in response to stimulation, and is pink with blue extremities. What is the APGAR score?", "a": ["5", "6", "7", "8"], "e": "The APGAR score assesses a newborn's condition at 1 and 5 minutes post-delivery based on five criteria: heart rate, respiratory effort, muscle tone, reflex irritability, and skin color. In this case, the baby has a heart rate of 90 bpm (1 point), weak crying (1 point), some flexion of the arms and legs (1 point), grimacing (1 point), and pink body with blue extremities (1 point). This gives a total APGAR score of 5. The baby is showing moderate distress and may need additional interventions like stimulation or assisted breathing.", "s": "working", "t": "Neonatal care"}, {"c": "FE-0029", "q": "You are present during the delivery of a baby who is not breathing spontaneously. After 1 minute of stimulation, the infant’s heart rate is 50 bpm, there is no muscle tone, and the skin is blue all over. What is your immediate action after determining the APGAR score?", "a": ["Provide bag-valve-mask ventilation and reassess", "Provide blow-by oxygen and stimulate the baby again", "Begin chest compressions immediately", "Administer 100% oxygen via non-rebreather mask"], "e": "In this situation, where the infant's heart rate is 50 bpm, there is no muscle tone, and the skin is blue after 1 minute of stimulation, the immediate action is to provide bag-valve-mask ventilation and reassess. A heart rate below 100 in a newborn requires intervention, and a heart rate below 60 typically requires chest compressions. However, since the heart rate is 50 and the baby isn't breathing spontaneously, ventilating with a BVM is the most effective way to provide oxygen and stimulate breathing. Blow-by oxygen or a non-rebreather mask would not deliver enough oxygen, and chest compressions aren't the first step unless the heart rate remains below 60 after ventilation.", "s": "working", "t": "Neonatal care"}, {"c": "FE-0030", "q": "You respond to a 25-year-old complaining of sudden, sharp lower abdominal pain on the right side. She denies any recent trauma, has no significant medical history, and her last menstrual period was 10 days ago. She reports no vaginal bleeding. Her vitals are stable, and she is alert but in pain. What is the most likely cause of her symptoms?", "a": ["Ovarian cyst rupture", "Ectopic pregnancy", "Preeclampsia", "Placenta previa"], "e": "The most likely cause of the patient’s sudden, sharp lower abdominal pain with no significant medical history or recent trauma is ovarian cyst rupture. Ovulation usually occurs approximately 10 days post mensturation, and an ectopic pregnancy usually takes a missed period plus a week or two. Ovarian cysts are common and can rupture, causing sudden pain, particularly on one side of the lower abdomen. Ectopic pregnancy is less likely given the absence of missed periods or other pregnancy-related symptoms. Preeclampsia and placenta previa are both related to pregnancy, which she denies.", "s": "working", "t": "Bleeding"}, {"c": "FE-0031", "q": "A 30-year-old woman is complaining of severe lower abdominal pain and vaginal bleeding. She states her period is 6 weeks late, and she has been feeling lightheaded. Her vitals are BP 90/60, HR 120, and RR 22. She denies any history of trauma. What condition should you be most concerned about?", "a": ["Ectopic pregnancy", "Ovarian cyst rupture", "Urinary tract infection", "Appendicitis"], "e": "Given the patient’s severe lower abdominal pain, vaginal bleeding, and signs of shock (BP 90/60, HR 120), the most concerning condition is ectopic pregnancy. This condition occurs when a fertilized egg implants outside the uterus, usually in a fallopian tube, and can lead to life-threatening internal bleeding. The missed period and lightheadedness further suggest pregnancy complications. Other options like ovarian cyst rupture or appendicitis are less likely to present with vaginal bleeding.", "s": "working", "t": "Bleeding"}, {"c": "FE-0032", "q": "A 28-year-old presents with dull, intermittent lower abdominal pain and mild spotting. She reports a positive pregnancy test but is unsure how far along she is, as her periods are irregular. Her vitals are stable, and she has no significant medical history. What condition should you consider, and what is the most appropriate next step?", "a": ["Suspect ectopic pregnancy, provide rapid transport, and monitor for shock", "Suspect an ovarian cyst, provide reassurance, and advise follow-up with an OB-GYN", "Suspect normal pregnancy, advise prenatal vitamins, and obtain a refusal", "Suspect miscarriage, provide emotional support, and rapid transport"], "e": "The patient’s symptoms of dull, intermittent lower abdominal pain, spotting, and a positive pregnancy test raise suspicion of an ectopic pregnancy, especially given the irregular periods. Although her vitals are stable, the possibility of an ectopic pregnancy is a medical emergency, and the next step is to provide rapid transport and monitor for shock. Reassurance and follow-up are important, but they do not address the potential life-threatening nature of an ectopic pregnancy.", "s": "working", "t": "Gynecology"}, {"c": "FE-0033", "q": "You respond to a 32-year-old pregnant woman who is 30 weeks along and complaining of a headache and visual disturbances. Her blood pressure is 160/100, and she has swelling in her hands and feet. What condition should you suspect?", "a": ["Preeclampsia", "Eclampsia", "Gestational diabetes", "Hyperemesis gravidarum"], "e": "With symptoms of headache, visual disturbances, and a blood pressure of 160/100 at 30 weeks pregnant, the most likely condition is preeclampsia. Preeclampsia is characterized by high blood pressure, swelling, and organ damage, and if left untreated, it can progress to eclampsia, which involves seizures. The symptoms described do not align with gestational diabetes or hyperemesis gravidarum, which involve different signs.", "s": "working", "t": "Obstetrics"}, {"c": "FE-0034", "q": "You are called to the home of a 28-year-old woman who is 34 weeks pregnant. Her husband states she suddenly began seizing and has no history of seizures. Upon arrival, you find her postictal with labored breathing and swelling in her extremities. What should you suspect, and what is your next action?", "a": ["Suspect eclampsia; provide airway management, monitor vitals, and prepare for rapid transport", "Suspect epilepsy; provide oxygen and allow the seizure to resolve", "Suspect placental abruption; assess for vaginal bleeding and transport", "Suspect preeclampsia; place supine with legs raised and begin transport"], "e": "The most likely diagnosis in this scenario is eclampsia, as the patient experienced a seizure with no prior history of seizures and is 34 weeks pregnant. Eclampsia is the progression of preeclampsia to seizures. The next step is to provide airway management, monitor vitals, and prepare for rapid transport to ensure the patient is stabilized and to prevent further complications. This is a life-threatening emergency that requires immediate attention and hospital care.", "s": "working", "t": "Bleeding"}, {"c": "FE-0035", "q": "A 26-year-old woman, 36 weeks pregnant, is experiencing severe headaches, blurred vision, and high blood pressure. Her OB-GYN diagnosed her with preeclampsia. She is being transported to the hospital for induction of labor to prevent further complications. During transport, she begins to develop twitching in her hands and arms. What is the best action to take during transport? ", "a": ["Administer oxygen and position the patient on her left side", "Insertion of an OPA immediately and begin assisting ventilations", "Provide oral glucose in case of hypoglycemia", "Place the patient supine and elevate her legs"], "e": "Since the patient has preeclampsia and is now developing twitching in her hands and arms, this suggests that she may be progressing toward eclampsia. The best action is to administer oxygen and position the patient on her left side. This position improves blood flow to the placenta and reduces the risk of seizures. Inserting an OPA or administering glucose is unnecessary unless the patient becomes unresponsive or there is another indication for those interventions.", "s": "working", "t": "Obstetrics"}, {"c": "FE-0036", "q": "You are called to the home of a 29-year-old woman who delivered a baby 3 days ago. She complains of a severe headache and nausea. Upon examination, her blood pressure is 170/110, and she is experiencing blurred vision. She denies chest pain or shortness of breath. What should you suspect, and how should you proceed?", "a": ["Suspect postpartum preeclampsia; transport rapidly to the hospital", "Suspect postpartum hemorrhage; monitor vitals and transport non-emergently", "Suspect postpartum eclampsia; transport rapidly to the hospital", "Suspect postpartum ovarian cyst rupture; transport non-emergently to the hospital"], "e": "With symptoms of severe headache, nausea, blurred vision, and high blood pressure (170/110) just 3 days after delivery, the most likely condition is postpartum preeclampsia. This condition can develop after childbirth and requires immediate medical attention to prevent progression to eclampsia. The appropriate response is to transport rapidly to the hospital for evaluation and treatment. Postpartum hemorrhage and ovarian cyst rupture would present with different symptoms, and transport should not be delayed in this situation.", "s": "working", "t": "Obstetrics"}, {"c": "FE-0037", "q": "You respond to a 45-year-old who is choking at a restaurant. He is clutching his throat and cannot speak, but he is still able to cough weakly. His skin is turning blue around the lips. What is your next step?", "a": ["Encourage the patient to continue coughing and monitor", "Administer abdominal thrusts immediately", "Perform back blows and chest thrusts", "Place the patient in a recovery position"], "e": "Since the patient is still able to cough, even though weakly, the best course of action is to encourage the patient to continue coughing and monitor. A weak cough indicates that the airway is still partially open, and coughing is the most effective way for the body to expel the obstruction. Intervening with abdominal thrusts or back blows at this point could worsen the situation by pushing the object further into the airway. Only if the patient becomes unable to cough or breathe should more aggressive actions be taken.", "s": "working", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-0038", "q": "You arrive at the home of a 60-year-old woman who is complaining of shortness of breath. She has a history of COPD, and her respiratory rate is 34, with shallow breaths. You notice cyanosis around her lips and extremities. What is your next step?", "a": ["Assist her ventilations using a bag-valve mask (BVM)", "Administer 100% oxygen via non-rebreather mask", "Provide nasal cannula oxygen at 2 L/min", "Position her supine and monitor for improvement"], "e": "Given the patient's history of COPD, assisting her ventilations using a bag-valve mask (BVM) is the next step. The respiratory rate of 34 with shallow breaths, combined with cyanosis, indicates that she is not ventilating effectively and is in respiratory distress. A non-rebreather mask could be helpful, but because her breaths are too shallow, she requires positive pressure ventilation to support her breathing. EMTs can assist ventilations with a BVM within their scope of practice.", "s": "working", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-0039", "q": "You are called to a home where a 3-year-old boy is having difficulty breathing. He has a barking cough and stridor, and his mother says he has had a cold for several days. What is your first action?", "a": ["Administer oxygen if tolerated and keep the child calm", "Administer high-flow oxygen via non-rebreather mask", "Administer humidified oxygen", "Suction the airway for possible mucus obstruction"], "e": "With signs of croup (barking cough and stridor), the first action is to administer oxygen if tolerated and keep the child calm. Croup involves upper airway swelling, and keeping the child calm is critical to avoid worsening the airway obstruction. Administering high-flow oxygen or aggressively suctioning could increase distress, making the airway situation worse. Oxygen should be provided gently if the child will tolerate it. Humidified oxygen is no longer recommended per NASEMSO.", "s": "working", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-0040", "q": "A 35-year-old woman is having an asthma attack. She is struggling to breathe, using accessory muscles, and unable to complete sentences. What is your immediate action?", "a": ["Assist the patient with taking her metered-dose inhaler after calling medical control", "Administer oxygen via nasal cannula and call medical control", "Encourage the patient to take slow, deep breaths and position her upright", "Provide bag-valve mask ventilations and prepare for transport"], "e": "In this scenario, the patient is having an asthma attack and struggling to breathe. The immediate action is to assist the patient with taking her metered-dose inhaler after calling medical control. The inhaler is the most effective treatment to relieve bronchoconstriction. Administering oxygen or encouraging breathing exercises won’t address the underlying issue as effectively as the inhaler. If the patient’s condition worsens, further interventions like BVM ventilations may be needed, but the inhaler is the first step.", "s": "working", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-0041", "q": "A 6-year-old boy was choking on a piece of food and has now become unresponsive. After calling for ALS backup, what should you do next?", "a": ["Begin chest compressions and check the airway for the object", "Perform abdominal thrusts until the object is expelled", "Perform a blind finger sweep to remove the object", "Attempt to deliver rescue breaths immediately"], "e": "When the child becomes unresponsive after choking, the next action is to begin chest compressions and check the airway for the object. Chest compressions can help dislodge the object, and checking the airway allows you to visually inspect for anything that could be removed. Abdominal thrusts and blind finger sweeps are not recommended once the patient is unresponsive, as they could cause additional harm. Chest compressions are more effective in these cases.", "s": "working", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-0042", "q": "You respond to a 15-year-old with a known peanut allergy. She is experiencing difficulty breathing, wheezing, and swelling of the lips after consuming a snack at a party. Her blood pressure is 80/50, and her skin is pale. What is your next action?", "a": ["Provide oxygen therapy while you contact medical control to administer epinephrine", "Administer an antihistamine and transport immediately", "Provide high-flow oxygen via non-rebreather mask and transport", "Begin bag-valve mask ventilations and prepare for ALS backup"], "e": "The 15-year-old is experiencing anaphylaxis, which is life-threatening, as evidenced by difficulty breathing, wheezing, swelling, and hypotension (80/50). The immediate action is to provide oxygen therapy while contacting medical control to administer epinephrine. Epinephrine is the first-line treatment for anaphylaxis, and EMTs can administer it with authorization. Oxygen supports the patient’s breathing, but epinephrine is critical to reversing the allergic reaction. Antihistamines alone are not fast-acting enough in this situation.", "s": "working", "t": "Immunology"}, {"c": "FE-0043", "q": "You are called to a school where a 4-year-old girl is sitting upright, drooling, and making high-pitched noises when breathing in. Her mother says she had a fever the night before and was diagnosed with strep throat earlier in the week. What should you do next?", "a": ["Provide calm reassurance, oxygen, and transport rapidly", "Suction her airway to clear any obstructions", "Perform a head-tilt, chin-lift to open the airway", "Administer albuterol and monitor her respiratory rate"], "e": "The 4-year-old girl is showing signs of epiglottitis, which can cause airway obstruction. The next step is to provide calm reassurance, oxygen, and transport rapidly. Epiglottitis is a life-threatening condition that requires immediate intervention in a hospital setting. Agitation or invasive procedures like suctioning could worsen the airway swelling. The goal is to keep the child calm and ensure that oxygen is provided as much as tolerated while getting her to definitive care. Humidified oxygen is not recommended per NASEMSO.", "s": "working", "t": "Airway Management"}, {"c": "FE-0044", "q": "You are called to a house fire where a 50-year-old man is coughing and having difficulty breathing after being trapped inside for several minutes. His face is covered in soot, and he has burns around his mouth. What is your first action?", "a": ["Administer high-flow oxygen via non-rebreather mask", "Suction his airway to clear the soot and provide humidified oxygen", "Call for a paramedic to intubate the patient and secure the airway", "Provide bag-valve mask ventilations to assist with his breathing"], "e": "Given the patient’s exposure to a fire, administering high-flow oxygen via non-rebreather mask is the first action. Inhalation of smoke and toxic gases can cause serious respiratory issues, and high-flow oxygen is essential to support breathing and prevent hypoxia. Although the patient may need advanced airway management, including intubation, administering high-flow oxygen is the immediate priority to improve oxygenation while awaiting additional care.", "s": "working", "t": "Ventilation"}, {"c": "FE-0045", "q": "A 6-month-old infant is experiencing respiratory distress. He has nasal flaring, grunting, and is breathing rapidly. His pulse oximetry shows 88% oxygen saturation. What is your next course of action?", "a": ["Assist ventilations using a bag-valve mask", "Provide high-flow oxygen via non-rebreather mask", "Position the infant in a supine position and monitor", "Perform chest compressions and ventilate immediately"], "e": "For a 6-month-old infant with respiratory distress, nasal flaring, grunting, and an oxygen saturation of 88%, the next action is to assist ventilations using a bag-valve mask (BVM). Infants have small airways and can deteriorate quickly, and the low oxygen saturation (88%) suggests that the child is not ventilating adequately. High-flow oxygen via a non-rebreather mask might not deliver enough support due to the infant's shallow breathing. The BVM ensures the infant receives adequate ventilation.", "s": "working", "t": "Airway Management"}, {"c": "FE-0046", "q": "You respond to the scene of a car accident where a 25-year-old has severe head trauma and is unconscious. He is breathing shallowly and irregularly. What is your next action to manage his airway?", "a": ["Perform a jaw-thrust maneuver and assist ventilations with a bag-valve mask", "Administer oxygen via nasal cannula and monitor closely", "Insert an oropharyngeal airway and perform chest compressions", "Tilt his head back and open his airway with a head-tilt chin-lift maneuver"], "e": "For the 25-year-old with severe head trauma, shallow, irregular breathing, the next step to manage the airway is to perform a jaw-thrust maneuver and assist ventilations with a bag-valve mask (BVM). The jaw-thrust maneuver protects the cervical spine, which could be injured due to the trauma. Using a head-tilt, chin-lift could worsen a potential spinal injury. Assisting with ventilations is necessary because the patient’s breathing is insufficient. Nasal cannula or oxygen alone is not adequate for this level of respiratory distress.", "s": "working", "t": "Multi-System Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-0047", "q": "You respond to a call for a 40-year-old involved in a car crash. He was wearing a seatbelt, but the airbags deployed. He complains of severe chest pain, difficulty breathing, and you note bruising across his chest from the seatbelt. His lung sounds are diminished on the left side, and his pulse is 110 bpm. What is your immediate action?", "a": ["Provide oxygen and monitor for shock", "Perform a needle decompression", "Provide positive pressure ventilation and transport rapidly", "Immobilize the patient on a backboard and provide high-flow oxygen"], "e": "The patient’s presentation (chest pain, diminished breath sounds, and bruising from the seatbelt) suggests a possible pneumothorax, so the immediate action is to provide positive pressure ventilation and transport rapidly. This will help maintain adequate oxygenation, especially if the lung has collapsed. A needle decompression is outside the EMT scope of practice and should only be performed by ALS providers. Oxygen should be provided, but the patient also requires rapid transport due to the potential for the condition to worsen.", "s": "working", "t": "Chest Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-0048", "q": "You are called to a construction site where a 28-year-old has suffered an open fracture of his left forearm. The bone is protruding through the skin, and the patient is in severe pain. What is your immediate priority?", "a": ["Cover the exposed bone with a sterile dressing and splint the arm", "Realign the bone and splint the arm", "Apply a tourniquet above the fracture to control bleeding", "Apply direct pressure to the wound and immobilize the arm"], "e": "In the case of an open fracture where the bone is protruding through the skin, the immediate priority is to cover the exposed bone with a sterile dressing and splint the arm. This helps prevent infection and further injury while immobilizing the arm to reduce pain. You should not attempt to realign the bone, and a tourniquet is not necessary unless there is severe, uncontrolled bleeding.", "s": "working", "t": "Bleeding"}, {"c": "FE-0049", "q": "You are called to a playground where a 12-year-old boy fell off a jungle gym, hitting his head. He is confused, has a large bump on his head, and is complaining of a headache. He is alert and has no other visible injuries. What is your next course of action?", "a": ["Immobilize the cervical spine and transport rapidly", "Apply an ice pack to his head and observe for 15 minutes", "Perform a neurological assessment and monitor for seizure activity", "Allow him to walk to the ambulance and monitor for dizziness"], "e": "Since the 12-year-old has head trauma, confusion, and a large bump on his head, the next course of action is to immobilize the cervical spine and transport rapidly. Head trauma, especially with confusion, raises concerns for a concussion or more serious traumatic brain injury. Immobilization ensures that any potential spinal injuries are protected, and rapid transport allows for further evaluation. Allowing the child to walk or merely observing him is not appropriate in this scenario.", "s": "working", "t": "Head, Facial, neck, and Spine Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-0050", "q": "You are called to a kitchen where a 35-year-old woman has spilled boiling water on her chest and arms. The skin is red, blistered, and extremely painful. What is your next course of action?", "a": ["Cover the burns with clean, dry dressings and transport", "Apply a burn ointment and bandage the area", "Apply cool, wet dressings and transport rapidly", "Apply a cold pack to the burned areas and monitor vital signs"], "e": "For a patient with partial-thickness burns (red, blistered skin) on the chest and arms, the next course of action is to cover the burns with clean, dry dressings and transport. This helps protect the burned area from infection. Wet dressings or cold packs can cause hypothermia, especially over large areas of the body. Burn ointments are also outside of an EMT’s scope for prehospital care.", "s": "working", "t": "Soft tissue Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-0051", "q": "You respond to a 27-year-old who was stabbed in the abdomen during a fight. He is conscious but pale and diaphoretic, with a rapid pulse and shallow breathing. Blood is seeping from the wound around the impaled object. What is your next step?", "a": ["Stabilize the impaled object in place to prevent further injury", "Remove the impaled object and apply direct pressure to control bleeding", "Remove the knife and apply a tourniquet above the wound", "Cover the wound with an occlusive dressing and prepare for rapid transport"], "e": "When a patient has an impaled object in the abdomen, the next step is to stabilize the impaled object in place to prevent further injury. Removing the object could cause more damage and lead to uncontrolled bleeding. The goal is to minimize movement of the object while preparing for rapid transport to a hospital for definitive care and surgery.", "s": "working", "t": "Bleeding"}, {"c": "FE-0052", "q": "A 25-year-old has fallen from a ladder and is complaining of severe back pain. He is unable to move his legs and reports tingling in his arms. What is your immediate action?", "a": ["Immobilize the spine and transport rapidly", "Perform a rapid extrication and immobilize the spine", "Assist the patient to a standing position and assess mobility", "Apply a backboard and check for circulation in the extremities"], "e": "For a patient who has fallen from a height and is experiencing severe back pain, with inability to move the legs and tingling in the arms, the immediate action is to immobilize the spine and transport rapidly. These are classic signs of a spinal cord injury, and rapid immobilization is crucial to prevent further damage to the spinal cord. Moving the patient or attempting to assess mobility could worsen the injury.", "s": "working", "t": "Head, Facial, neck, and Spine Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-0053", "q": "A 40-year-old has had his right hand amputated in an industrial accident. The wound is bleeding heavily, and the patient is in shock. What is your priority after ensuring scene safety?", "a": ["Apply a tourniquet above the amputation site to control bleeding", "Apply direct pressure to the wound and wrap the severed hand in a sterile dressing", "Pack the wound with sterile gauze and elevate the arm", "Place the severed hand in ice water and prepare for rapid transport"], "e": "In the case of a hand amputation with severe bleeding, the priority is to apply a tourniquet above the amputation site to control bleeding. Heavy bleeding from an amputation can quickly lead to shock, and a tourniquet is the fastest and most effective way to stop the bleeding in this scenario. Direct pressure or packing the wound may not be sufficient for an amputation. The severed hand should be cared for after bleeding control, but applying the tourniquet takes precedence.", "s": "working", "t": "Bleeding"}, {"c": "FE-0054", "q": "You respond to the scene of a multi-vehicle collision. A 50-year-old has multiple injuries, including an open fracture of the femur, abdominal bruising, and slow bleeding from a large laceration on his forehead. He is conscious but disoriented and in severe pain. What should your initial assessment focus on?", "a": ["Assessing and managing the airway, breathing, and circulation", "Immobilizing the femur and treating the fracture", "Controlling the bleeding from the laceration and applying a pressure dressing", "Applying a cervical collar and monitoring for signs of shock"], "e": "In the setting of multiple traumatic injuries, including an open femur fracture, abdominal bruising, and a laceration, your initial assessment should focus on assessing and managing the airway, breathing, and circulation (ABCs). This is the primary step in trauma care, as addressing life-threatening airway, breathing, or circulation issues is more critical than focusing on specific injuries like fractures or lacerations. Once the ABCs are stable, further interventions like controlling bleeding or immobilizing fractures can follow.", "s": "working", "t": "Head, Facial, neck, and Spine Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-0055", "q": "You are called to a 23-year-old who was hit in the head with a baseball bat during a fight. He is confused, vomiting, and has a slow, irregular pulse. His pupils are unequal, and his blood pressure is rising. What condition should you suspect?", "a": ["Traumatic brain injury with increasing intracranial pressure", "Concussion with no loss of consciousness", "Hypovolemic shock due to head trauma", "Spinal cord injury with neurogenic shock"], "e": "The patient's symptoms of confusion, vomiting, unequal pupils, slow pulse, and rising blood pressure suggest traumatic brain injury (TBI) with increasing intracranial pressure. These signs indicate the brain is swelling or bleeding internally, which can compress vital structures. A concussion would typically not present with such severe signs, and hypovolemic shock from head trauma is less likely with these vital signs. The focus should be on rapid transport to a trauma center for further care.", "s": "working", "t": "Neurology"}, {"c": "FE-0056", "q": "A 28-year-old is having difficulty breathing after being stung by a bee. Her face is swollen, and she has hives across her chest. Her blood pressure is 90/60, and she is wheezing. What treatments will provide the patient with the most benefit?", "a": ["Assisting the patient with their epinephrine auto-injector and provide high-flow oxygen", "Provide oxygen via nasal cannula and administer antihistamines", "Begin bag-valve mask ventilations and prepare for intubation", "Administer albuterol via nebulizer and monitor for improvement"], "e": "For the patient experiencing anaphylaxis after a bee sting, with swelling, hives, wheezing, and hypotension, the most beneficial treatment is to assist the patient with their epinephrine auto-injector and provide high-flow oxygen. Epinephrine is the first-line treatment for anaphylaxis, as it helps reverse the airway constriction and low blood pressure. Oxygen supports breathing, but antihistamines or albuterol are not sufficient to address the severity of this reaction.", "s": "working", "t": "Immunology"}, {"c": "FE-0057", "q": "A 45-year-old with a history of diabetes is complaining of abdominal pain, excessive thirst, and frequent urination. He is confused, and his breath smells fruity. His blood sugar reads 400 mg/dL. What is the most likely condition?", "a": ["Diabetic ketoacidosis", "Hypoglycemia", "Gastrointestinal bleeding", "Myocardial infarction"], "e": "With symptoms of abdominal pain, excessive thirst, frequent urination, confusion, fruity-smelling breath, and a blood sugar of 400 mg/dL, the most likely condition is diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA). This is a serious complication of diabetes, where the body starts breaking down fat too quickly, leading to high levels of ketones in the blood. Hypoglycemia would present with a low blood sugar, and gastrointestinal bleeding or myocardial infarction would have different presenting symptoms.", "s": "working", "t": "Endocrine Disorders"}, {"c": "FE-0058", "q": "You arrive at a park where a 24-year-old has just finished seizing. He is in the postictal phase, confused, and breathing rapidly. His vital signs are stable. What is your next step?", "a": ["Monitor airway, breathing, and circulation, and transport", "Administer an anti-seizure medication and transport rapidly", "Begin chest compressions in case of cardiac arrest", "Provide oral glucose in case of a diabetic emergency"], "e": "After a seizure, the patient is in the postictal phase, which is a period of confusion and exhaustion following the seizure. The next step is to monitor airway, breathing, and circulation, and transport. Postictal patients often recover fully on their own, but airway and breathing should be monitored closely. There is no need for anti-seizure medications or chest compressions unless the patient deteriorates. Providing glucose is unnecessary unless there are signs of hypoglycemia.", "s": "working", "t": "Neurology"}, {"c": "FE-0059", "q": "A 30-year-old has called 911 experiencing shortness of breath and is using accessory muscles to breathe. He has a history of asthma and is unable to complete sentences. What should you do first upon arrival on scene?", "a": ["Establish scene safety", "Assist the patient with an albuterol inhaler", "Provide high-flow oxygen and prepare for transport", "Position the patient supine and monitor breathing"], "e": "Upon arriving to treat a patient with shortness of breath and asthma, the first step is to assist the patient with an albuterol inhaler. Asthma is characterized by airway constriction, and albuterol is the most effective medication to relieve the symptoms quickly. High-flow oxygen is important if the patient’s oxygen levels are low, but assisting with the inhaler takes priority to open the airways. Positioning the patient supine could worsen breathing difficulties, so it should be avoided.", "s": "working", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-0060", "q": "You are called to a soccer field where a 20-year-old has collapsed after playing in the heat for 2 hours. He is sweating profusely, weak, and complaining of dizziness. His skin is hot to the touch, and his pulse is rapid. What should you do?", "a": ["Move him to a cool area, provide fluids, and monitor his condition", "Apply ice packs to his body and immerse him in cold water", "Administer high-flow oxygen and rapidly transport", "Administer oral glucose and monitor for changes"], "e": "The patient’s symptoms of profuse sweating, weakness, dizziness, hot skin, and rapid pulse suggest heat exhaustion, potentially progressing to heat stroke. The correct action is to move him to a cool area, provide fluids, and monitor his condition. Heat exhaustion occurs from prolonged exposure to heat and dehydration. Cooling the patient and providing fluids are critical to prevent the progression to heat stroke, which is life-threatening. Immersing the patient in cold water is appropriate only if heat stroke is suspected, but based on the symptoms, fluid replacement and cooling are the priorities.", "s": "working", "t": "Monitoring Devices"}, {"c": "FE-0061", "q": "You arrive at the home of a 25-year-old who is unresponsive. His friend states that he injected heroin about 30 minutes ago. The patient is breathing slowly, with a pulse of 40 bpm, and his pupils are pinpoint. Your service does carry naloxone for EMTs to administer. What actions will benefit the patient the most?", "a": ["Provide assisted ventilations and administer naloxone", "Begin chest compressions and provide high-flow oxygen", "Place the patient in a recovery position and monitor breathing", "Administer naloxone and prepare for rapid transport"], "e": "For a patient who is unresponsive, breathing slowly, has a pulse of 40 bpm, and pinpoint pupils, after a heroin overdose, the next action is to provide assisted ventilations and administer naloxone. Assisted ventilations are necessary due to the patient’s slow breathing, and naloxone (Narcan) is administered to reverse the effects of the opioid. While naloxone can help, airway management and ensuring adequate ventilation are crucial, as respiratory depression is the main cause of death in opioid overdoses.", "s": "working", "t": "Toxicology"}, {"c": "FE-0062", "q": "A 4-year-old child is found by her parents with an open bottle of pills. She is conscious but lethargic and has vomited twice. The parents do not know how many pills she ingested or what type. What is your next step?", "a": ["Call poison control and transport the child to the hospital", "Administer activated charcoal and monitor vital signs", "Induce vomiting and transport the child rapidly", "Monitor for signs of shock and administer high-flow oxygen"], "e": "In the case of a 4-year-old found with an open bottle of pills, who is conscious but lethargic and has vomited, the next step is to call poison control and transport the child to the hospital. Poison control can provide guidance on how to proceed based on the type and quantity of pills ingested. Activated charcoal may be administered based on their recommendations, but inducing vomiting or administering charcoal without knowing the substance could be harmful.", "s": "working", "t": "Toxicology"}, {"c": "FE-0063", "q": "You respond to a factory where a chemical spill has occurred. Two workers are down inside the spill zone. No hazmat team is present, and there are fumes in the air. What is your immediate course of action?", "a": ["Request additional resources, secure the scene, and prevent entry", "Don appropriate PPE and enter the spill zone to assess the workers", "Begin decontaminating the workers with water and provide oxygen", "Move the workers out of the spill zone for treatment"], "e": "In the event of a chemical spill where there are fumes present and no hazmat team on the scene, the immediate course of action is to request additional resources, secure the scene, and prevent entry. It is crucial to avoid entering the spill zone without proper PPE and training, as you could become a victim. Securing the perimeter and waiting for the hazmat team is the safest approach, as they will handle decontamination and removal from the spill zone.", "s": "working", "t": "Hazardous Materials"}, {"c": "FE-0064", "q": "You are the first responder at the scene of a multi-car pileup involving 10 vehicles. Several people are injured, but resources are limited. What is your first action when managing this mass casualty incident?", "a": ["Perform triage and categorize patients based on severity", "Begin treating the most critically injured patient first", "Call for additional ambulances and wait for backup", "Treat the patients who can walk away from the scene"], "e": "In a mass casualty incident (MCI) involving multiple vehicles, your first action is to perform triage and categorize patients based on severity. This allows you to prioritize treatment based on who needs immediate life-saving interventions and who can wait for care. Treating the most critically injured first is only appropriate after the initial triage process. Calling for additional resources should happen concurrently, but triage comes first to ensure the most efficient use of limited resources. You would not wait for additional ambulances prior to starting triage.", "s": "working", "t": "Incident Management"}, {"c": "FE-0065", "q": "You respond to a rollover car accident where one patient is critically injured, and another has minor injuries. You need to update dispatch with a patient report. What critical information should you provide first?", "a": ["The number of patients and severity of injuries", "The patient’s name, vital signs, and age", "Your estimated time of arrival to the hospital", "The mechanism of injury and details of the accident"], "e": "When updating dispatch at the scene of a rollover car accident involving multiple patients, the most critical information to provide first is the number of patients and severity of injuries. This helps dispatch allocate the appropriate resources and determine how many ambulances may be required. Details like patient names, vital signs, or the mechanism of injury are secondary to this essential information.", "s": "working", "t": "Scene Assessment"}, {"c": "FE-0066", "q": "A 60-year-old involved in a car accident refuses treatment and transport. He is alert and oriented but has visible abrasions and complains of neck pain. What should you do next?", "a": ["Document the refusal, explain the risks, and obtain a signed refusal form", "Contact law enforcement and force him to be transported", "Respect his decision and leave the scene", "Transport the patient against his will under implied consent"], "e": "When a 60-year-old patient refuses treatment and transport but has visible injuries and complaints of neck pain, your next step is to document the refusal, explain the risks, and obtain a signed refusal form. As long as the patient is alert and oriented, they have the right to refuse care. You should thoroughly explain the potential consequences of refusing transport and ensure that the refusal is properly documented. Transporting the patient against their will is not legal unless the patient is unable to make informed decisions.", "s": "working", "t": "Medical/Legal and Ethics"}, {"c": "FE-0067", "q": "You are transporting a 50-year-old with chest pain to the hospital. You need to notify the emergency department of your arrival. What information should you include in your radio report?", "a": ["Estimated time of arrival, patient’s condition, and treatments provided", "Patient’s history of diabetes, medications, and allergies", "Patient’s insurance information and medical history", "The hospital’s anticipated patient load and staffing needs"], "e": "When notifying the emergency department of your arrival with a 50-year-old patient with chest pain, your radio report should include the estimated time of arrival, the patient’s condition, and the treatments provided. This gives the hospital the critical information they need to prepare for the patient’s arrival. Details like medical history or insurance information can be handled once the patient arrives but are not necessary in the initial report.", "s": "working", "t": "Documentation"}, {"c": "FE-0068", "q": "After transporting a 45-year-old with a suspected stroke to the hospital, you need to complete your patient care report (PCR). What should be included in your documentation?", "a": ["A detailed account of the care provided and the patient’s response", "The patient’s diagnosis and discharge instructions from the hospital", "The time the patient was discharged from the hospital", "The hospital’s interpretation of your initial assessment"], "e": "After transporting a 45-year-old with a suspected stroke, your patient care report (PCR) should include a detailed account of the care provided and the patient’s response. The PCR serves as the legal documentation of your care and provides continuity for hospital staff. It should include the treatments administered, patient’s vital signs, and how the patient responded to interventions. The hospital’s diagnosis or discharge instructions are not part of the EMT’s documentation.", "s": "working", "t": "Documentation"}, {"c": "FE-0069", "q": "While transporting a patient, you receive a phone call from the patient's family asking for updates on their condition. The patient is unconscious, and you have not received prior consent to share their information. What should you do?", "a": ["Inform the family that you cannot share any details due to confidentiality", "Provide the family with a full update on the patient’s condition", "Ask the hospital for permission to share the patient’s information", "Provide the family with the patient’s condition but omit sensitive details"], "e": "If you receive a phone call from the patient's family asking for updates on their condition while the patient is unconscious and you have no prior consent to share information, the correct response is to inform the family that you cannot share any details due to confidentiality. Patient privacy laws, such as HIPAA, prevent healthcare providers from sharing a patient's health information without explicit consent, even with family members. You should explain that you are unable to provide updates without the patient’s prior approval. While they may be family, it does not mean that they are family the patient would like to know what is going on. ", "s": "working", "t": "Medical/Legal and Ethics"}, {"c": "FE-0070", "q": "You are called to the home of an 80-year-old who is in cardiac arrest. His daughter presents you with a valid Do Not Resuscitate (DNR) order. What should you do next?", "a": ["Respect the DNR order and provide comfort to family", "Begin CPR while verifying the DNR order", "Contact medical control for permission to withhold CPR", "Refuse to follow the DNR order until you confirm with law enforcement"], "e": "When presented with a valid Do Not Resuscitate (DNR) order for a patient in cardiac arrest, you should respect the DNR order and provide comfort care. A valid DNR order means that the patient has chosen not to receive resuscitation efforts, such as CPR. Your role is to provide care aimed at comfort, such as managing pain or anxiety, while honoring their legal wishes. Initiating CPR or contacting law enforcement would go against the patient’s directive.", "s": "working", "t": "Medical/Legal and Ethics"}, {"c": "FE-0071", "q": "You are transporting a critical trauma patient to the hospital with lights and sirens. While en route, you approach an intersection with heavy traffic. What is the safest way to proceed?", "a": ["Stop at the intersection, ensure it is clear, and proceed with caution", "Increase your speed and proceed through the intersection quickly", "Proceed through the intersection at a normal speed with sirens on", "Turn off your lights and sirens to avoid alarming other drivers"], "e": "When approaching an intersection with heavy traffic while using lights and sirens, the safest way to proceed is to stop at the intersection, ensure it is clear, and proceed with caution. This helps avoid collisions and ensures that other drivers have time to respond to your presence. Speeding through or proceeding without stopping can be dangerous, as other drivers may not be aware of your approach.", "s": "working", "t": "Transport Decision"}, {"c": "FE-0072", "q": "You are the incident commander at the scene of a building collapse with multiple casualties. Resources are limited, and more units are on the way. What should you prioritize while waiting for additional resources?", "a": ["Assign personnel to triage patients and allocate available resources", "Begin treating the most critically injured patients immediately", "Attempt to extricate trapped patients while waiting for more help", "Send all walking patients to the nearest hospital on foot"], "e": "At the scene of a building collapse with multiple casualties, while waiting for additional resources, your priority should be to assign personnel to triage patients and allocate available resources. Triage is the process of determining the priority of patients based on the severity of their injuries. This allows you to use limited resources effectively and ensures that those in the most critical condition receive care as soon as possible.", "s": "working", "t": "Incident Management"}, {"c": "FE-0073", "q": "You respond to a 4-year-old who is struggling to breathe. His mother reports he has been coughing with a fever for the past three days and today began wheezing. On arrival, you find the child sitting in a tripod position, breathing rapidly with audible wheezing. What is your first action?", "a": ["Administer oxygen", "Administer albuterol", "Perform back blows and abdominal thrusts", "Assess for a foreign body airway obstruction"], "e": "For a 4-year-old struggling to breathe with wheezing and a fever, the first action should be to administer oxygen. This child is showing signs of respiratory distress, and oxygen will help improve their breathing. Administering albuterol should only be done after consulting medical control, and back blows or abdominal thrusts would not be appropriate unless there is a clear indication of an airway obstruction, which is not present here.", "s": "working", "t": "Airway Management"}, {"c": "FE-0074", "q": "A 75-year-old has fallen at home and is complaining of severe hip pain. She is unable to move her right leg, which appears shortened and externally rotated. Her vitals are stable, but she is in significant pain. What is your immediate course of action?", "a": ["Immobilize her leg in the position found and provide pain relief", "Attempt to realign the leg before splinting", "Transport immediately without attempting to move the leg", "Apply a traction splint and transport rapidly"], "e": "For the 75-year-old woman with a suspected hip fracture, where the leg is shortened and externally rotated, the immediate course of action is to immobilize her leg in the position found and provide pain relief. Attempting to realign the leg could cause further injury, and applying a traction splint is not appropriate for this type of injury. Immobilizing the leg and preparing for transport is the safest approach.", "s": "working", "t": "Orthopedic Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-0075", "q": "You are assisting in the delivery of a baby when you notice that the baby’s buttocks are presenting first. The mother is in active labor and pushing. What is your next step?", "a": ["Support the baby’s body and allow for natural delivery of the head", "Apply firm pressure to the mother’s abdomen to assist with delivery", "Instruct the mother to stop pushing and wait for ALS assistance", "Attempt to reposition the baby and prepare for rapid transport"], "e": "When the baby’s buttocks are presenting first (breech presentation) during delivery, the next step is to support the baby’s body and allow for natural delivery of the head. Breech births require careful handling to avoid complications, and attempting to reposition the baby or instructing the mother to stop pushing is not advised. ALS should be called for assistance, but you should continue to support the delivery as much as possible.", "s": "working", "t": "Obstetrics"}, {"c": "FE-0076", "q": "A 6-year-old is experiencing a seizure at school. Her teacher reports that she was feeling ill earlier in the day and had a low-grade fever. The seizure lasts for two minutes, and the child is now postictal. What should you do next?", "a": ["Monitor the child’s airway and breathing, and transport for further evaluation", "Administer oral glucose and wait for her to regain full consciousness", "Provide high-flow oxygen and transport her non-emergently", "Induce vomiting in case of ingested toxins"], "e": "For a 6-year-old postictal after a seizure that lasted for two minutes, the next step is to monitor the child’s airway and breathing, and transport for further evaluation. The seizure may have been caused by a fever (febrile seizure), but regardless of the cause, it’s important to ensure the child’s airway remains clear and that she is breathing adequately during the postictal phase. Oral glucose is not indicated unless hypoglycemia is suspected, and inducing vomiting is inappropriate.", "s": "working", "t": "Neurology"}, {"c": "FE-0077", "q": "A 78-year-old is complaining of chest pain and shortness of breath. His vital signs are BP 150/90, HR 100, and RR 24. He is diaphoretic and anxious. His medical history includes hypertension and angina. What condition should you suspect?", "a": ["Myocardial infarction", "Hypoglycemia", "Hyperglycemia", "Cardiogenic shock"], "e": "Given the symptoms of chest pain, shortness of breath, sweating, and a history of hypertension and angina, the condition you should suspect is myocardial infarction (MI). The elevated BP and HR, along with the patient’s anxiety and diaphoretic state, are common signs of an MI. Hypoglycemia or hyperglycemia would present with different symptoms, and cardiogenic shock would typically involve lower blood pressure.", "s": "working", "t": "Cardiovascular"}, {"c": "FE-0078", "q": "A 29-year-old woman who is 34 weeks pregnant is complaining of a severe headache, visual disturbances, and swelling in her hands and feet. Her blood pressure is 160/100. What condition are you most suspicious of?", "a": ["Preeclampsia", "Gestational diabetes", "Eclampsia", "Preterm labor"], "e": "For a pregnant woman at 34 weeks with a severe headache, visual disturbances, swelling, and a blood pressure of 160/100, the condition most likely is preeclampsia. Preeclampsia involves high blood pressure and other signs of organ dysfunction and can lead to eclampsia if untreated. Gestational diabetes does not present with these symptoms, and preterm labor would typically present with abdominal pain and contractions.", "s": "working", "t": "Obstetrics"}, {"c": "FE-0079", "q": "A 5-year-old boy fell off a jungle gym and is complaining of pain in his arm. You observe swelling and deformity at the elbow. His pulse and capillary refill are normal, but he is in significant pain. What is your next step?", "a": ["Splint the arm in the position found and transport rapidly", "Attempt to realign the arm and apply a splint", "Apply ice and monitor for signs of shock", "Manipulate the arm into a normal position and splint in place"], "e": "For the 5-year-old boy with a suspected elbow fracture, your next step is to splint the arm in the position found and transport rapidly. Attempting to realign the arm or manipulate it into a normal position can cause further damage. Applying ice can help reduce swelling, but the priority is immobilization and transport to prevent further injury and address the pain.", "s": "working", "t": "Orthopedic Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-0080", "q": "A 70-year-old woman is found unresponsive by her family. They report that she recently started a new pain medication. Her respirations are shallow, and her pulse is 50 bpm. Her pupils are pinpoint. Your protocols dictate you must call medical control before administering medications. What is your immediate action?", "a": ["Assist ventilations and call medical control to administer naloxone", "Begin chest compressions and provide high-flow oxygen", "Place the patient in a recovery position and monitor for improvement", "Assist ventilations and call medical control to administer oral glucose"], "e": "For a 70-year-old woman who is unresponsive with shallow respirations, a pulse of 50 bpm, and pinpoint pupils after starting a new pain medication, the immediate action is to assist ventilations and call medical control to administer naloxone. The pinpoint pupils and shallow breathing suggest an opioid overdose, and naloxone can reverse this. Assisting with ventilations is critical to prevent respiratory arrest while waiting for further instructions from medical control.", "s": "working", "t": "Toxicology"}, {"c": "FE-0081", "q": "A 10-year-old is having difficulty breathing and has swelling around his face after eating peanut butter. His skin is pale, and his pulse is 120 bpm. You do have medications in your jump-bag but must call medical control before you use them. What is your next course of action?", "a": ["Assist the patient with their epi-pen auto injector", "Place the patient in the recovery position and begin rapid transport", "Provide high-flow oxygen and encourage him to drink water", "Call medical control to assist the patient with his metered-dose inhaler"], "e": "For a 10-year-old with swelling around his face and difficulty breathing after consuming peanut butter, the next course of action is to assist the patient with their epi-pen auto injector if available. This is a classic presentation of anaphylaxis, and epinephrine is the most effective treatment. High-flow oxygen should be provided, but drinking water or using a metered-dose inhaler is not appropriate for treating anaphylaxis.", "s": "working", "t": "Immunology"}, {"c": "FE-0082", "q": "You respond to a call for a 30-year-old found unconscious after spending the night outdoors in freezing temperatures. His skin is cold and pale, and his pulse is weak and slow. He is unresponsive to verbal stimuli. What is the treatment priority?", "a": ["Move the patient to a warm environment and provide passive rewarming", "Provide active rewarming with heat packs and warm fluids", "Rub the patient’s skin vigorously to warm him up", "Begin chest compressions due to weak pulse"], "e": "For a patient found unconscious after exposure to freezing temperatures, the treatment priority is to move the patient to a warm environment and provide passive rewarming. Passive rewarming involves covering the patient with blankets and allowing their body to gradually warm up. Active rewarming with heat packs can cause dangerous shifts in circulation, and vigorous rubbing of the skin can lead to tissue damage in hypothermic patients. Compressions should only be performed on patients who present without a pulse.", "s": "working", "t": "Environmental emergencies"}, {"c": "FE-0083", "q": "A 24-year-old collapsed after running a marathon on a hot day. He is unconscious, with hot, dry skin. His pulse is rapid, and his breathing is shallow. What should you do first?", "a": ["Move him to a cooler environment and begin active cooling with cold packs", "Provide high-flow oxygen and cover him with blankets to prevent shock", "Administer oral fluids and place him in the recovery position", "Initiate active cooling with wet towels and transport immediately"], "e": "For a 24-year-old unconscious with hot, dry skin after running a marathon, the immediate action is to move him to a cooler environment and begin active cooling with cold packs. The patient is showing signs of heat stroke, a life-threatening condition, and active cooling is necessary to reduce his core temperature as quickly as possible. High-flow oxygen is important, but covering him with blankets would increase his body temperature, worsening the condition.", "s": "working", "t": "Environmental emergencies"}, {"c": "FE-0084", "q": "A 65-year-old with a history of diabetes is complaining of fever, weakness, and confusion. Her skin is hot and flushed, and her blood pressure is 90/60. She reports recent treatment for a urinary tract infection. What should you be most suspicious for?", "a": ["Septic Shock", "Myocardial infarction", "Accidental overdose", "Ischemic stroke"], "e": "Given the patient’s fever, weakness, confusion, hot flushed skin, and recent treatment for a UTI, you should be most suspicious of septic shock. The low blood pressure (90/60) and history of infection indicate that the infection may have spread systemically, leading to sepsis. Septic shock occurs when an infection triggers widespread inflammation, leading to dangerously low blood pressure and organ dysfunction. A myocardial infarction or stroke would not typically present with these symptoms, and an overdose is less likely given the context.", "s": "working", "t": "Pathophysiology"}, {"c": "FE-0085", "q": "You arrive at the scene of a 60-year-old complaining of chest pain and shortness of breath. His skin is cool and clammy, and his blood pressure is 80/50. He has a history of heart disease and is currently taking heart medications. What should you be most suspicious for?", "a": ["Cardiogenic shock", "Septic shock", "Obstructive shock", "Psychogenic shock"], "e": "For the patient with chest pain, shortness of breath, cool clammy skin, and a blood pressure of 80/50, you should be most suspicious of cardiogenic shock. This condition occurs when the heart is unable to pump enough blood to meet the body’s needs, often due to heart disease or a heart attack. The patient’s history of heart disease and low blood pressure strongly suggest that the heart is the source of the issue. Septic shock or obstructive shock would have different underlying causes, and psychogenic shock wouldn’t cause these symptoms.", "s": "working", "t": "Pathophysiology"}, {"c": "FE-0086", "q": "You respond to a call for a 20-year-old who has locked herself in her room and has expressed suicidal thoughts to her friend. On arrival, she is calm but refuses to leave the room or go to the hospital. What should you do?", "a": ["Contact law enforcement and stay with the patient until they arrive", "Leave the scene and allow her to calm down", "Transport the patient against her will for mental health evaluation", "Provide reassurance and encourage her to follow up with a counselor"], "e": "When responding to a suicidal patient who refuses to leave the room or go to the hospital, your first step should be to contact law enforcement and stay with the patient until they arrive. In situations where a patient is a danger to themselves, law enforcement can help ensure the patient receives a mental health evaluation, even if they refuse. Leaving the scene or transporting the patient against their will without law enforcement involvement is not advised.", "s": "working", "t": "Psychiatric or Behavioral Emergencies"}, {"c": "FE-0087", "q": "You are called to the home of a 28-year-old who is hyperventilating and complaining of chest pain. He is visibly anxious and states that he feels like he is going to die. His vital signs are normal, and there are no signs of trauma. What is your immediate action?", "a": ["Encourage slow, deep breaths and provide reassurance", "Administer oxygen and prepare for rapid transport", "Call for law enforcement officers", "Advise the patient that he has a good presentation and obtain a refusal"], "e": "For the 28-year-old who is hyperventilating, complaining of chest pain, and visibly anxious, your immediate action is to encourage slow, deep breaths and provide reassurance. The patient is likely experiencing a panic attack or anxiety-related hyperventilation. Reassuring the patient and helping them control their breathing will help reduce the symptoms. Administering oxygen is generally not necessary unless the patient is hypoxic, and there is no need for law enforcement in this case as there is no indication of risk of violence.", "s": "working", "t": "Psychiatric or Behavioral Emergencies"}, {"c": "FE-0088", "q": "You are called to the home of a 75-year-old with a history of congestive heart failure. She is complaining of shortness of breath, and presents with rales as well as swollen ankles. Her skin is pale and cool, and she is coughing up frothy sputum. What condition is she currently presenting with?", "a": ["Pulmonary edema", "Renal failure", "COPD exacerbation", "Obstructive shock"], "e": "For the 75-year-old with shortness of breath, rales (crackles), swollen ankles, and frothy sputum, the condition she is presenting with is pulmonary edema, likely due to congestive heart failure (CHF). CHF leads to fluid buildup in the lungs (pulmonary edema), which causes difficulty breathing, crackles in the lungs, and frothy sputum. Her swollen ankles (edema) further suggest fluid overload, a common symptom of CHF.", "s": "working", "t": "Cardiovascular"}, {"c": "FE-0089", "q": "A 22-year-old is found unresponsive at a party. Friends report that he drank heavily over the past several hours. His respirations are slow and shallow with vomit in his airway, and his pulse is weak. You do have medications in your jump-bag that you can use. What is your immediate action?", "a": ["Suction the airway and assess for a gag reflex", "Administer nalaxone with medical control approval", "Insertion of an oropharyngeal airway and assist ventilations", "Place the patient in the recovery position and provide assisted ventilations"], "e": "For the 22-year-old unresponsive after heavy drinking, with slow, shallow respirations and vomit in his airway, your immediate action should be to suction the airway and assess for a gag reflex. Clearing the airway is the priority, as vomit can obstruct breathing and lead to aspiration. After suctioning, assisting ventilations or inserting an airway device may be necessary depending on the patient’s level of responsiveness. Naloxone is not indicated here unless opioid use is suspected, and placing the patient in the recovery position without addressing the airway would not be sufficient.", "s": "working", "t": "Airway Management"}, {"c": "FE-0090", "q": "You respond to a call at a home where two family members have been found unconscious in a garage with a running car. They are breathing shallowly, and their skin is cherry red. What should you do first?", "a": ["Immediately leave the area and call for additional resources", "Ventilate the garage and wait for the patients to wake up", "Have you and your partner assist one patient each with positive pressure ventilation", "Place the patients in the recovery position and vacate the area until additional resources arrive"], "e": "For the unconscious family members found in a garage with a running car and cherry-red skin, your first step is to immediately leave the area and call for additional resources. This presentation is consistent with carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning, which can be deadly. You and your partner should avoid further exposure to the toxic environment, and you must wait for specialized resources (such as a hazmat team) to safely enter the area and treat the patients. Ventilating the garage yourself or attempting to treat the patients inside without the proper equipment is too dangerous.", "s": "working", "t": "Rescue Operations"}, {"c": "FE-0091", "q": "You respond to a motor vehicle accident where a 30-year-old has severe facial trauma and is unconscious. His respirations are shallow, and there are signs of blood in the mouth. What is your immediate action?", "a": ["Perform a jaw-thrust maneuver and prepare to suction the airway", "Perform a head-tilt chin-lift to open the airway", "Suction the airway and perform a head-tilt chin lift manuever", "Perform a jaw-thrust maneuver and assess for a gag reflex"], "e": "For a patient with severe facial trauma, unconsciousness, shallow respirations, and blood in the mouth, the immediate action is to perform a jaw-thrust maneuver and prepare to suction the airway. The jaw-thrust maneuver is used to open the airway while protecting the cervical spine, which could be injured due to the trauma. Suctioning the airway is critical to remove the blood and prevent aspiration. A head-tilt chin-lift should be avoided in trauma patients due to the risk of spinal injury.", "s": "working", "t": "Bleeding"}, {"c": "FE-0092", "q": "You arrive at a home where a 55-year-old is found unresponsive. Bystanders are performing high-quality CPR and you have confirmed pulselessness. What should you do first?", "a": ["Attach the AED and analyze the patient’s rhythm", "Take over CPR and administer high-flow oxygen", "Perform a rapid trauma assessment before starting CPR", "Have bystanders pause compressions while you reassess for a pulse."], "e": "When arriving at a scene where high-quality CPR is already being performed, and you have confirmed the patient is pulseless, your first action is to attach the AED and analyze the patient’s rhythm. This will determine whether the patient has a shockable rhythm, allowing for the administration of a life-saving defibrillation if indicated. CPR should continue until the AED is attached and ready to analyze.", "s": "working", "t": "Resuscitation"}, {"c": "FE-0093", "q": "During a mass casualty incident, you encounter a patient who is responsive, has a patent airway, can follow commands, but has a fracture in the left leg and moderate bleeding. Under the SALT triage system, which category does this patient fall into?", "a": ["Yellow", "Red", "Green", "Black"], "e": "Under the SALT triage system, a patient who is responsive, has a patent airway, can follow commands, but has a fracture and moderate bleeding falls into the Yellow category. The Yellow category indicates delayed care, meaning the patient is stable enough to wait for treatment, but still needs attention. This patient is not in immediate life-threatening danger, but the injury requires treatment once more urgent cases have been handled.", "s": "working", "t": "Multiple Casualty Incidents"}, {"c": "FE-0094", "q": "Which color code typically indicates a patient who needs immediate care during triage?", "a": ["Red", "Green", "Yellow", "Black"], "e": "In triage systems, the Red color code typically indicates a patient who needs immediate care. Patients classified as Red are in critical condition and need rapid intervention to survive. They have life-threatening injuries but are expected to survive if treated promptly.", "s": "working", "t": "Multiple Casualty Incidents"}, {"c": "FE-0095", "q": "During a mass casualty incident, you encounter an adult patient who is unresponsive, has rapid and shallow breathing at a rate of 40 breaths per minute, and does not obey commands. Under the START triage system, which category does this patient fall into?", "a": ["Red", "Green", "Yellow", "Black"], "e": "Under the START triage system, a patient who is unresponsive, breathing rapidly and shallowly at 40 breaths per minute, and not following commands should be categorized as Red. The Red category indicates immediate care is needed, as the patient’s rapid breathing and unresponsiveness suggest a life-threatening condition. This patient is at high risk of decompensating without urgent treatment.", "s": "working", "t": "Multiple Casualty Incidents"}, {"c": "FE-0096", "q": "Which of the following establishes a difference between a tort and a criminal action in EMS?", "a": ["Torts involve private disputes; criminal actions involve public offenses", "Torts seek monetary compensation; criminal actions seek punishment", "Torts always lead to imprisonment; criminal actions do not", "Torts require a trial by jury; criminal actions do not"], "e": "Torts involve private disputes and seek monetary compensation, while criminal actions involve public offenses and seek punishment, establishing the key differences. The incorrect options state that torts always lead to imprisonment, which they do not, and that torts require a trial by jury, which is not necessarily true for all cases, unlike criminal actions where these points do not apply universally.", "s": "working", "t": "Medical/Legal and Ethics"}, {"c": "FE-0097", "q": "How is telemetry used in EMS?", "a": ["To transmit patient vital signs and ECG data to receiving hospitals", "For patient billing processes and insurance claims", "For navigation purposes and route planning", "To transmit insurance information to the hospital"], "e": "Telemetry is used in EMS to transmit patient vital signs and ECG data to receiving hospitals, enabling real-time monitoring and preparation for the patient's arrival. It is not used for billing, insurance claims, or navigation purposes.", "s": "working", "t": "Monitoring Devices"}, {"c": "FE-0098", "q": "What is the primary responsibility of an EMT when operating an emergency vehicle?", "a": ["Ensuring the safety of the crew, patient, and the public", "Arriving at the scene as quickly as possible", "Using lights and sirens at all times", "Following GPS directions without deviation"], "e": "The primary responsibility of an EMT when operating an emergency vehicle is ensuring the safety of the crew, patient, and the public. Speed, constant use of lights and sirens, and strict adherence to GPS directions are less important than maintaining safety.", "s": "working", "t": "Public Health Overview"}, {"c": "FE-0099", "q": "What is a common goal of public health that EMTs support?", "a": ["Reducing the incidence of preventable diseases", "Increasing the number of emergency calls", "Promoting longer hospital admissions", "Limiting access to health services"], "e": "A common goal of public health that EMTs support is reducing the incidence of preventable diseases by promoting health education, vaccination, and preventive care. Increasing emergency calls, promoting longer hospital admissions, and limiting access to health services do not align with public health goals.", "s": "working", "t": "Public Health Overview"}, {"c": "FE-0100", "q": "What is a method employers can provide employees to manage stress?", "a": ["Providing critical incident stress debriefings (CISD)", "Encouraging silent reflection and meditation practices", "Scheduling more shifts to keep employees busy", "Offering gym memberships and fitness programs"], "e": "A method employers can provide employees to manage stress is providing critical incident stress debriefings (CIS, which help EMTs process traumatic events and reduce the risk of long-term stress-related issues. Encouraging silent reflection, and scheduling more shifts,  can be helpful but are more likely to increase negative emotions long-term. Offering gym memberships can be nice, but is not as effective as professional intervention after a traumatic event. ", "s": "working", "t": "Workforce Safety and Wellness"}, {"c": "FE-0101", "q": "You are responding to a 44-year-old male in cardiac arrest. Upon arrival, you confirm pulselessness while your partner establishes vascular access and secures an advanced airway. Other responders are providing chest compressions, and you ask one of them to begin ventilating the patient via a bag-valve-mask (BVM) connected to the advanced airway. You notice that the responder is delivering a breath once every 3 seconds and is squeezing the bag completely with each ventilation. What is the effect of hyperventilating a patient in cardiac arrest?", "a": ["Hyperventilation has been shown to lead to reduced coronary perfusion", "Hyperventilation has been shown to lead to increased coronary perfusion", "Hyperventilation has been shown to improve the chances of return of spontaneous circulation (ROSC)", "Hyperventilation has been shown to prevent coronary perfusion"], "e": "Hyperventilating a patient during cardiac arrest can be harmful because it increases intrathoracic pressure, which reduces venous return to the heart and decreases coronary perfusion pressure. Delivering breaths too rapidly or with excessive volume can impede blood flow during CPR, ultimately reducing the chances of successful resuscitation. The recommended ventilation rate for an adult patient with an advanced airway during CPR is one breath every 6 seconds (10 breaths per minute), using only enough volume to see visible chest rise. Therefore, hyperventilation leads to reduced coronary perfusion, negatively impacting the patient's outcome.", "s": "working", "t": "Resuscitation"}, {"c": "FE-0102", "q": "You are responding to a 5-year-old drowning victim. Upon arrival, the family is pulling the child out of the lake onto the shore. It is suspected the child was submerged for approximately 5 minutes. You confirm pulselessness and apnea and begin CPR with your team. The child is estimated to weigh 45 lbs (about 20 kg). You have delivered one shock, established an intraosseous (IO) line in the left tibia, administered two doses of epinephrine, and started a bag of normal saline. Your partner notices that the posterior portion of the patient's leg is swelling. What is the next best course of action?", "a": ["Discontinue use of the infiltrated IO and establish secondary IO access if IV access is unobtainable", "Continue to use the infiltrated IO until other access can be obtained", "Apply a tourniquet to the left leg to prevent complications", "Administer the fluids through the advanced airway"], "e": "Swelling at the IO site indicates infiltration, meaning fluids and medications are leaking into the surrounding tissue rather than entering the circulation. Continuing to use an infiltrated IO can cause tissue damage and compartment syndrome. The appropriate action is to discontinue use of the infiltrated IO immediately. If intravenous (IV) access is not obtainable, establish a secondary IO in a different location to continue administering life-saving medications and fluids. Applying a tourniquet is inappropriate and can cause further harm. Administering IO fluids through an advanced airway is ineffective and not recommended. Therefore, the best course of action is to stop using the infiltrated IO and establish alternative access.", "s": "working", "t": "Resuscitation"}, {"c": "FE-0103", "q": "You respond to a 60-year-old male patient experiencing chest pain. He is diaphoretic, has cool, clammy skin, and reports radiating pain to his jaw. His vital signs are: BP 140/90 mmHg, HR 88 bpm, SpO₂ 95%, and RR 18 breaths per minute. He denies any allergies but does take atorvastatin and sildenafil daily. What is your next best step?", "a": ["Administer 324 mg of aspirin orally and assess for contraindications before considering nitroglycerin", "Administer 0.4 mg of nitroglycerin sublingually immediately", "Withhold aspirin and nitroglycerin and establish IV access for fluid administration", "Place the patient on supplemental oxygen via nasal cannula at 2 LPM"], "e": "We can reasonably suspect that this patient may be experiencing a myocardial infarction based on his chest pain radiating to the jaw, diaphoresis, and cool, clammy skin. Administering 324 mg of aspirin orally is appropriate because aspirin inhibits platelet aggregation, which is crucial in suspected acute coronary syndrome to prevent the clot from worsening. Since the patient has no allergies, aspirin is indicated. However, before administering nitroglycerin, it's essential to assess for contraindications like recent sildenafil (Viagra) use, which the patient takes daily; combining nitroglycerin with sildenafil can cause severe hypotension due to their vasodilatory effects. Administering nitroglycerin immediately without this assessment risks patient safety. Withholding aspirin and focusing on IV fluids delays critical care, and providing supplemental oxygen isn't necessary since his SpO₂ is 95%, which is adequate without signs of hypoxia. Unnecessary oxygen administration can lead to vasoconstriction and potentially worsen ischemia.", "s": "working", "t": "Cardiovascular"}, {"c": "FE-0104", "q": "You arrive at a home where a 4-year-old boy is experiencing difficulty breathing. He is wheezing, has nasal flaring, and is sitting in a tripod position. His mother states he has a history of asthma but hasn’t had an attack in months. His vital signs are: HR 110 bpm, RR 30 breaths per minute, SpO₂ 94% on room air. What is your immediate course of action?", "a": ["Administer a nebulized treatment of albuterol and monitor for tachycardia", "Administer a nebulized treatment of epinephrine and monitor for tachycardia", "Place the patient on a non-rebreather at 15 LPM and begin transport to the closest pediatric facility", "Start a treatment of humidified oxygen and consider epinephrine IM"], "e": "The 4-year-old boy is exhibiting signs of an acute asthma attack: wheezing, nasal flaring, and tripod positioning, with vital signs indicating tachypnea and tachycardia. Administering a nebulized treatment of albuterol and monitoring for tachycardia is appropriate, as albuterol is a bronchodilator that relieves bronchospasm—the immediate cause of his breathing difficulty. Monitoring for tachycardia is important due to albuterol's side effects. Administering nebulized epinephrine is typically reserved for upper airway obstructions like croup, not asthma and is also not within the AEMT scope of practice. Placing the patient on a non-rebreather at 15 LPM isn't immediately necessary since his SpO₂ is 94%; high-flow oxygen without bronchodilation won't alleviate bronchospasm. Starting humidified oxygen and considering epinephrine IM doesn't address the urgent need for bronchodilation in asthma.", "s": "working", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-0105", "q": "You arrive at a park where a 45-year-old woman is found incoherent. A bystander says she has a history of diabetes and was complaining of feeling weak before collapsing. Her skin is pale and clammy, and her blood glucose level is 40 mg/dL. IV access has been established. What is the most appropriate treatment?", "a": ["Administration of 25 G of dextrose via IV infusion", "Administration of a 20 ml/kg fluid bolus via IV infusion", "Administration of oral glucose buccally", "Administration of 1 mg of glucagon via IM injection"], "e": "The 45-year-old woman is incoherent with a blood glucose level of 40 mg/dL. Since IV access has been established, administering 25 grams of dextrose intravenously is the most appropriate treatment. IV dextrose provides a rapid increase in blood glucose levels, which is crucial for restoring her mental status. Administering a 20 ml/kg fluid bolus doesn't address the hypoglycemia. Oral glucose is contraindicated in an incoherent patient due to the risk of aspiration. Administering 1 mg of glucagon via IM injection is an alternative when IV access isn't available but is slower and less effective than IV dextrose.", "s": "working", "t": "Endocrine Disorders"}, {"c": "FE-0106", "q": "At a park, a 6-year-old girl is having an asthma attack. She is wheezing, struggling to breathe, and has circumoral cyanosis. Her mother cannot find their prescribed albuterol inhaler. The child's vital signs are: HR 120 bpm, RR 30 breaths per minute, SpO₂ 85%. What action will provide the most immediate benefit?", "a": ["Administer a nebulized treatment of albuterol", "Contact medical control to request orders for epinephrine administration", "Place the patient in the recovery position and begin assisting ventilations", "Provide humidified oxygen while preparing for transport"], "e": "The 6-year-old girl is experiencing a severe asthma attack, evidenced by wheezing, struggling to breathe, circumoral (area around the face and mouth) cyanosis, and a low SpO₂ of 85%. Administering a nebulized treatment of albuterol provides the most immediate benefit by relieving bronchospasm and improving oxygenation. Contacting medical control to request epinephrine may delay essential treatment; epinephrine isn't first-line therapy for asthma unless it's severe and unresponsive to albuterol. Placing her in the recovery position and beginning assisted ventilations might be necessary if her condition deteriorates, but initial treatment should focus on bronchodilation. Providing humidified oxygen while preparing for transport supports oxygenation but doesn't address the underlying bronchospasm causing her distress.", "s": "working", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-0107", "q": "A 6-year-old girl at a birthday party suddenly develops hives, difficulty breathing, and swelling of her lips after eating cake containing peanuts. She has a known peanut allergy but left her epinephrine auto-injector at home. She weighs 20 kg. What should you do?", "a": ["Administer epinephrine 0.2 mg (0.01 mg/kg) intramuscularly in the lateral thigh", "Administer epinephrine 2 mg (0.1 mg/kg) intramuscularly", "Administer epinephrine 0.5 mg intramuscularly", "Begin an epinephrine intravenous infusion of 0.15 mg over 15 minutes"], "e": "The patient is showing signs of anaphylaxis after ingesting peanuts—hives, difficulty breathing, and lip swelling. She weighs 20 kg, and the appropriate dose of epinephrine is 0.01 mg/kg, totaling 0.2 mg administered intramuscularly in the lateral thigh. Epinephrine is the first-line treatment for anaphylaxis, rapidly reversing airway swelling and hypotension. Administering 2 mg IM is ten times the recommended dose and could cause severe adverse effects. Administering 0.5 mg IM exceeds the maximum pediatric dose and is appropriate for adults, not children. Beginning an epinephrine IV infusion delays immediate treatment and isn't standard practice in the prehospital setting, while also breaking the scope of practice at the AEMT level.", "s": "working", "t": "Immunology"}, {"c": "FE-0108", "q": "A 35-year-old woman is having a severe asthma attack. She is struggling to breathe, using accessory muscles, and is unable to complete sentences. Her SpO₂ is 89% on room air. According to your protocols, you can administer medications without prior medical control approval but must call after initial medication administration. What action will bring the patient the most immediate benefit?", "a": ["Call medical control after administration of a nebulized treamtent of albuterol", "Administer a CPAP treatment starting at a pressure of 5 cm H2O", "Encourage the patient to take slow, deep breaths and position her upright", "Provide bag-valve mask ventilations and prepare for transport"], "e": "According to protocols, we can administer medications without prior medical control approval but must call after initial administration. Administering a nebulized treatment of albuterol promptly is essential to relieve bronchospasm and improve her breathing. Calling medical control after ensures compliance with protocols. Initiating CPAP isn't typically the first-line treatment in asthma and may not be tolerated, potentially making this patient much worse. Encouraging slow, deep breaths and positioning her upright may help but is insufficient without bronchodilator therapy. Providing bag-valve mask ventilations and preparing for transport may be necessary if her condition worsens but isn't the immediate next step.", "s": "working", "t": "Respiratory"}, {"c": "FE-0109", "q": "You respond to a 15-year-old girl with a known peanut allergy. She is experiencing difficulty breathing, wheezing, and swelling of the lips after consuming a snack at a party. Her blood pressure is 80/50 mmHg, HR 130 bpm, and her skin is pale and diaphoretic. What treatment plan would provide the most immediate benefit?", "a": ["Administer epinephrine 0.3 mg intramuscularly immediately and provide supplemental oxygen", "Contact medical control to request orders for an epinephrine intravenous infusion", "Establish IV access and begin a 20 mL/kg fluid bolus with normal saline", "Place the patient on a non-rebreather mask at 15 LPM and administer antihistamines"], "e": "The 15-year-old girl is exhibiting signs of anaphylactic shock: difficulty breathing, wheezing, lip swelling, hypotension (BP 80/50 mmHg), and tachycardia (HR 130 bpm). Administering epinephrine 0.3 mg intramuscularly immediately is crucial, as it reverses airway swelling and supports blood pressure. Providing supplemental oxygen helps improve oxygenation during respiratory distress. Contacting medical control to request an epinephrine IV infusion delays urgent care; intramuscular administration is faster and within the AEMT scope of practice. Establishing IV access and beginning a fluid bolus may support circulation but doesn't replace the immediate need for epinephrine. Administering antihistamines doesn't act quickly enough to address the life-threatening symptoms of anaphylaxis and is also not a standard part of the AEMT scope of practice.", "s": "working", "t": "Immunology"}, {"c": "FE-0110", "q": "A 28-year-old woman is having difficulty breathing after being stung by a bee. Her face is swollen, she has hives across her chest, and she is wheezing audibly. Her vital signs are: BP 90/60 mmHg, HR 110 bpm, RR 24 breaths per minute, SpO₂ 92%. What treatment will provide the most immediate benefit?", "a": ["Administer epinephrine 0.3 mg intramuscularly in the lateral thigh and provide high-flow oxygen", "Provide oxygen via nasal cannula at 4 LPM and administer diphenhydramine orally", "Begin assisted ventilations with a bag-valve mask and prepare for possible intubation", "Administer albuterol via nebulizer and monitor for improvement"], "e": "The 28-year-old woman is experiencing anaphylaxis after a bee sting, indicated by facial swelling, hives, audible wheezing, hypotension (BP 90/60 mmHg), and tachycardia (HR 110 bpm). Administering epinephrine is the most immediate and effective treatment, as it counteracts the severe allergic reaction by reversing vasodilation and bronchoconstriction. Providing high-flow oxygen addresses hypoxia resulting from respiratory compromise. Providing oxygen via nasal cannula at 4 LPM and administering diphenhydramine orally is insufficient for severe anaphylaxis and acts too slowly. Beginning assisted ventilations may be necessary if her condition worsens but should not delay epinephrine administration. Administering albuterol addresses bronchospasm but also doesn't treat the systemic effects of anaphylaxis.", "s": "working", "t": "Immunology"}, {"c": "FE-0111", "q": "You respond to a 4-year-old boy who is struggling to breathe. His mother reports he has had a cough and fever for the past three days and today began wheezing. On arrival, the child is sitting upright, breathing rapidly with audible wheezing. His vital signs are: HR 130 bpm, RR 36 breaths per minute, SpO₂ 90%. What action is most likely to benefit this patient?", "a": ["Start a nebulizer treatment of albuterol and monitor for improvement", "Start a nebulizer treatment of epinephrine and monitor for improvement", "Assess for laryngeal edema and administer an intramuscular injection of epinephrine", "Perform a finger sweep to check for a foreign body airway obstruction"], "e": "The 4-year-old boy is struggling to breathe with a history of cough and fever, now presenting with audible wheezing and an SpO₂ of 90%. Starting a nebulizer treatment of albuterol and monitoring for improvement is most likely to benefit him by relieving bronchospasm associated with asthma or bronchiolitis. Albuterol effectively relaxes the smooth muscles in the airways, improving airflow. Starting a nebulizer treatment of epinephrine is more appropriate for upper airway issues like croup, not lower airway wheezing - but that oes exceed the AEMT scope of practice. Assessing for laryngeal edema and administering epinephrine IM is unnecessary unless anaphylaxis is suspected, and the history does not suggest this is an acute reaction. Performing a finger sweep for a foreign body airway obstruction is inappropriate without signs of obstruction and could potentially cause injury.", "s": "working", "t": "Airway Management"}, {"c": "FE-0112", "q": "A 70-year-old woman is found unresponsive by her family. They report that she recently started a new opioid pain medication. Her respirations are shallow at 6 breaths per minute, her pulse is 50 bpm, and her pupils are pinpoint. Your protocols require you to contact medical control before administering medications. What is your immediate action?", "a": ["Begin assisted ventilations with a bag-valve mask and contact medical control for permission to administer naloxone", "Administer 0.4 mg of naloxone intravenously and continue ventilatory support", "Establish IV access and administer a 20 mL/kg fluid bolus of normal saline", "Place the patient in the recovery position and monitor vital signs closely"], "e": "This scenario is suggesting an opioid overdose with the new pain medication. Your protocols require contacting medical control before administering medications. Therefore, the immediate action is to begin assisted ventilations with a bag-valve mask to support her breathing and contact medical control for permission to administer naloxone, which can reverse the effects of the overdose. Administering naloxone without approval violates protocols, despite the indications for the medication. Establishing IV access and administering a fluid bolus doesn't address respiratory depression caused by the opioid. Placing the patient in the recovery position doesn't provide adequate ventilatory support in the face of respiratory failure.", "s": "working", "t": "Toxicology"}, {"c": "FE-0113", "q": "A 10-year-old boy is having difficulty breathing and has swelling around his face after eating peanut butter. His skin is pale, his pulse is 120 bpm, and he is wheezing. You have medications in your jump bag but must call medical control before administering them. The child has his own epinephrine auto-injector. What immediate action is most appropriate?", "a": ["Assist the patient in using his own epinephrine auto-injector immediately", "Administer 0.3 mg of epinephrine intramuscularly from your own supply", "Start a nebulized albuterol treatment while contacting medical control for orders", "Place the patient on high-flow oxygen and prepare to administer epinephrine intravenously"], "e": "This patient is experiencing anaphylaxis evidenced by difficulty breathing, facial swelling, pale skin, and wheezing. You have medications but must call medical control before administering them, and the child has his own epinephrine auto-injector. The most appropriate immediate action is to assist the patient in using his own epinephrine auto-injector immediately. This provides rapid administration of life-saving medication within protocol allowances. Administering 0.3 mg of epinephrine from your own supply requires medical control approval and could delay treatment. Starting a nebulized albuterol treatment while contacting medical control doesn't address the systemic effects of anaphylaxis promptly and still breaks your protocol to not giving medications prior to approval. Preparing to administer epinephrine intravenously is inappropriate for the AEMT scope of practice and has many treatments that should be performed before that is done.", "s": "working", "t": "Immunology"}, {"c": "FE-0114", "q": "You are called to treat a 60-year-old male complaining of chest pain and shortness of breath. His vitals are BP 160/90, HR 90 bpm, RR 20, and SpO₂ is 93%. He is not allergic to medications and has no history of taking erectile dysfunction drugs. What is your next course of action?", "a": ["Administer aspirin 324 mg orally and nitroglycerin 0.4 mg sublingually", "Administer nitroglycerin 0.4 mg sublingually", "Administer aspirin 324 mg orally", "Administer nitroglycerin 324 mg sublingually and aspirin 0.4 mg orally"], "e": "In this scenario, the 60-year-old male is experiencing chest pain and shortness of breath, with vital signs showing elevated blood pressure (160/90 mmHg) and an SpO₂ of 93%. He has no medication allergies and no history of taking erectile dysfunction drugs, which are contraindications for certain medications. Given these factors, the next appropriate course of action is to administer aspirin 324 mg orally and nitroglycerin 0.4 mg sublingually. Aspirin is crucial because it inhibits platelet aggregation, reducing the progression of a possible clot in acute coronary syndrome. Nitroglycerin aids by dilating the coronary arteries, improving blood flow to the heart muscle, and relieving chest pain. Administering both medications together addresses the underlying issue and provides symptomatic relief. Omitting either medication would result in less effective treatment of his condition.", "s": "working", "t": "Cardiovascular"}, {"c": "FE-0115", "q": "You arrive on scene to treat a 25-year-old female who is 36 weeks pregnant and in labor, and an ALS unit is en route. She is experiencing strong contractions, and upon inspection, you note that the baby’s head is crowning. What should your first action be?", "a": ["Prepare for an imminent delivery and assist the mother through childbirth", "Administer fluids to prevent dehydration and wait for paramedics to arrive", "Place the mother in the left lateral position and prepare for transport", "Administer oxygen and immediately initiate rapid transport"], "e": "The 25-year-old female is 36 weeks pregnant and in active labor, with strong contractions and the baby's head crowning. Crowning indicates that birth is imminent. The first action should be to prepare for an imminent delivery and assist the mother through childbirth. This involves creating a clean environment, supporting the baby's head during delivery, and being ready to provide care to the newborn. Delaying delivery to administer fluids, reposition the mother, or initiate transport is inappropriate because the baby will likely arrive before these actions can be completed safely. Immediate assistance ensures the safety and well-being of both the mother and the newborn.", "s": "working", "t": "Obstetrics"}, {"c": "FE-0116", "q": "You are treating a 4-year-old male patient with hypoglycemia who weighs 18 kg. You plan to administer Dextrose (D10) via IV at 5 ml/kg. What is the correct amount of Dextrose to administer to this pediatric patient?", "a": ["100 mL", "200 mL", "150 mL", "50 mL"], "e": "In pediatric patients with hypoglycemia, the standard dosage for Dextrose 10% (D10) is 5 mL/kg administered intravenously. For a 4-year-old child weighing 18 kg, the calculation is as follows:\n\nDose=5 mL/kg×18 kg=90 mL\n\nSince 90 mL is the exact calculated dose and the closest option provided is 100 mL, administering 100 mL of D10 is appropriate and within acceptable dosing limits. This amount effectively raises the blood glucose level to treat hypoglycemia without risking fluid overload or hyperglycemia.\n\nAdministering 150 mL or 200 mL would exceed the recommended dose, potentially causing adverse effects due to excessive fluid or glucose administration. Providing only 50 mL would be insufficient to correct the hypoglycemia in an 18 kg child.", "s": "working", "t": "Endocrine Disorders"}, {"c": "FE-0117", "q": "A 50-year-old male is involved in a motor vehicle accident and presents with decreased breath sounds on the left side, jugular venous distension, and tracheal deviation. His blood pressure is 80/60 mmHg, and his respiratory rate is 30. What is the most likely cause of his condition?", "a": ["Tension pneumothorax", "Aortic disection", "Cardiac tamponade", "Flail chest"], "e": "The 50-year-old male presents with decreased breath sounds on the left side, jugular venous distension (JVD), tracheal deviation, hypotension (BP 80/60 mmHg), and a respiratory rate of 30. These signs point to a tension pneumothorax. In this condition, air enters the pleural space and cannot escape, leading to increased intrathoracic pressure that compresses the lungs and shifts mediastinal structures, causing tracheal deviation and JVD. This impedes venous return to the heart, resulting in hypotension. Other conditions like aortic dissection, cardiac tamponade, or flail chest do not present with this combination of symptoms, making tension pneumothorax the most likely cause.", "s": "working", "t": "Chest Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-0118", "q": "You are transporting a 12-year-old patient who was involved in a severe motor vehicle accident. During transport, the patient’s vital signs deteriorate, showing signs of shock. You decide to administer a fluid bolus. What is the appropriate amount of normal saline to administer for this pediatric patient weighing 30 kg?", "a": ["600 mL", "1,000 mL", "300 mL", "200 mL"], "e": "For the 12-year-old patient weighing 30 kg showing signs of shock, the appropriate fluid bolus is calculated using 20 mL/kg:\n\n30 kg×20 mL/kg=600 mL\n\nTherefore, administering 600 mL of normal saline is appropriate to restore circulating volume and improve perfusion. Administering significantly more could risk fluid overload, while less may not be sufficient to address the hypovolemia and signs of shock.", "s": "working", "t": "Shock"}, {"c": "FE-0119", "q": "You are dispatched to a scene where a patient is experiencing severe respiratory distress. You have already administered albuterol and oxygen therapy, but the patient's condition is worsening; they are becoming fatigued and have decreased consciousness. What is your next step in airway management?", "a": ["Begin BVM ventilation with 100% oxygen", "Initiate CPAP at 5 cm H2O", "Administer epinephrine 0.3 mg intramuscularly", "Administer a second dose of albuterol via nebulizer"], "e": "When a patient's respiratory condition worsens despite initial treatments like albuterol and oxygen, and they begin to show signs of fatigue or decreased consciousness, the next critical step is to begin bag-valve-mask (BVM) ventilation with 100% oxygen. This intervention provides positive pressure ventilation, supporting or taking over the patient's breathing to ensure adequate oxygenation and ventilation.\n\nInitiating CPAP at 5 cm H₂O may not be appropriate if the patient cannot maintain their airway or is experiencing decreased consciousness, as CPAP requires the patient to be awake and able to follow commands. Administering epinephrine 0.3 mg intramuscularly is indicated for conditions like anaphylaxis, not for a patient whose respiratory distress is unresponsive to albuterol without signs of an allergic reaction.", "s": "working", "t": "Airway Management"}, {"c": "FE-0120", "q": "A 30-year-old male is struck in the chest by a heavy object and is now in severe respiratory distress. You notice paradoxical chest wall movement and bruising. What is the most likely diagnosis, and what intervention is needed?", "a": ["Flail chest; provide positive pressure ventilation with BVM", "Tension pneumothorax; needle decompression", "Diaphragmatic rupture; rapid transport to trauma center", "Pulmonary contusion; provide high-flow oxygen via non-rebreather"], "e": "The 30-year-old male struck in the chest exhibits paradoxical chest wall movement and bruising, which are classic signs of a flail chest. This condition occurs when a segment of the rib cage breaks under extreme stress and becomes detached from the rest of the chest wall. The appropriate intervention is to provide positive pressure ventilation with a BVM. Positive pressure stabilizes the flail segment, improves ventilation, and enhances oxygenation. None of the other conditions would directly lead to paradoxical chest wall movement. While they should be managed with oxygen therapy, the immediate concern here is the flail chest.", "s": "working", "t": "Chest Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-0121", "q": "A patient involved in a multi-car collision presents with muffled heart sounds, hypotension, and jugular venous distension. You suspect cardiac tamponade. What is the immediate intervention required for this condition?", "a": ["Provide high-flow oxygen and rapid transport", "Administer a fluid bolus to raise blood pressure", "Initiate needle decompression of the chest", "Begin chest compressions and ventilations"], "e": "The patient involved in a multi-car collision presents with muffled heart sounds, hypotension, and jugular venous distension—collectively known as Beck's triad—which are indicative of cardiac tamponade. The immediate intervention required is to provide high-flow oxygen and rapid transport to a facility capable of performing pericardiocentesis, the definitive treatment. While administering a fluid bolus might temporarily support blood pressure, it does not address the accumulating pericardial fluid compressing the heart. Needle decompression is indicated for tension pneumothorax, not cardiac tamponade, and beginning chest compressions is unnecessary as the patient is not in cardiac arrest.", "s": "working", "t": "Chest Trauma"}, {"c": "FE-0122", "q": "You are on scene at a mass casualty incident and have been assigned to triage. A 35-year-old female presents with altered mental status, absent radial pulses, and a respiratory rate of 35 breaths per minute. Using START triage, what triage category does this patient fall into?", "a": ["Red (Immediate)", "Yellow (Delayed)", "Green (Minor)", "Black (Deceased)"], "e": "In the mass casualty incident, an adult with altered mental status, absent radial pulses, and a respiratory rate of 35 breaths per minute falls into the Red (Immediate) triage category due to the altered mental status and absent radial pulses. This classification is for patients who have life-threatening conditions that require immediate intervention to survive.", "s": "working", "t": "Multiple Casualty Incidents"}, {"c": "FE-0123", "q": "You arrive at the scene of a mass casualty incident (MCI) and are assigned to triage. A 50-year-old male has a respiratory rate of 28 breaths per minute, capillary refill of 4 seconds, and is able to follow simple commands. Using START traige, what is his triage category?", "a": ["Yellow (Delayed)", "Red (Immediate)", "Green (Minor)", "Black (Deceased)"], "e": "This patient should be categorized as Yellow (Delayed) in START triage. As an adult the patient has a respiratory rate that is between 10 and 30, it does not mention absent distal pulses, and being able to follow simple commands indicates a mental status it present. While there is a chance of decompensation for this patient, as he presents now there is not an immediate concern to life, which is why this would be delayed. If there are no other patients who are more serious, then this patient will still be receiving treatment early - but other patients with a critical life threatening presentation should be cared for first. ", "s": "working", "t": "Multiple Casualty Incidents"}, {"c": "FE-0124", "q": "You are treating a 72-year-old female with suspected congestive heart failure (CHF). She presents with severe respiratory distress, crackles upon auscultation, and pedal edema. Her vital signs are BP 170/90, HR 110, and SpO₂ is 85%. What is the most appropriate treatment?", "a": ["Start CPAP at 10 cm H₂O pressure and monitor vitals", "Administer nitroglycerin and transport immediately", "Administer epinephrine 0.3 mg intramuscularly", "Administer albuterol via nebulizer"], "e": "This patient is presenting with most likely with congestive heart failure exacerbation. We can suspect this with the crackles and respiratory distress that has led to pulmonary edema. The most appropriate treatment is to start CPAP at 10 cm H₂O pressure and monitor vitals. CPAP helps to open collapsed alveoli, reduce the work of breathing, and improve oxygenation. It also decreases preload and afterload, which can alleviate the symptoms of CHF. Administering nitroglycerin could be beneficial but requires careful monitoring and may not be as immediately effective as CPAP in addressing her respiratory distress and is also not standard within the AEMT scope of practice. Epinephrine is contraindicated in CHF patients due to its cardiac stimulating effects, and albuterol is not indicated as it can increase heart rate and does not address fluid overload in the lungs and can make the pulmonary edema worse.", "s": "working", "t": "Cardiovascular"}, {"c": "FE-0125", "q": "You are transporting a 3-year-old male patient with signs of hypovolemic shock after a traumatic injury. He weighs 15 kg. What is the appropriate fluid bolus of normal saline to administer?", "a": ["300 mL", "500 mL", "200 mL", "100 mL"], "e": "For the 3-year-old male patient weighing 15 kg showing signs of hypovolemic shock after trauma, the appropriate fluid bolus of normal saline is calculated as 20 mL/kg:\n\n15 kg×20 mL/kg=300 mL\n\nTherefore, administering 300 mL of normal saline is suitable to replenish intravascular volume and improve perfusion. Providing 500 mL would exceed the recommended volume for a child of this weight and could risk fluid overload. Administering 200 mL or 100 mL may not be sufficient to effectively treat the hypovolemia and stabilize the patient.", "s": "working", "t": "Shock"}, {"c": "FE-0126", "q": "You are called to treat a 30-year-old male who was exposed to a hazardous chemical. You have donned appropriate PPE, and it is safe to enter the scene. He is experiencing respiratory distress, and his skin shows signs of chemical burns. What is the first step in managing this patient?", "a": ["Remove the patient from the hazardous environment and decontaminate", "Administer high-flow oxygen via non-rebreather mask", "Administer IV fluids to treat chemical exposure", "Provide CPAP to improve oxygenation"], "e": "The first step in managing a patient exposed to a hazardous chemical is to remove the patient from the hazardous environment and decontaminate. This action prevents further exposure to the chemical agent and reduces the risk of secondary contamination to rescuers and others. Decontamination involves removing contaminated clothing and thoroughly washing the skin to eliminate residual chemicals, thereby preventing ongoing absorption and additional injury.\n\nAdministering high-flow oxygen is important but should occur after decontamination to avoid contaminating equipment and exposing healthcare providers. Administering IV fluids and providing CPAP may be necessary later but are not the immediate priorities. Addressing the source of exposure and ensuring the safety of both the patient and responders is paramount before proceeding with further treatment.", "s": "working", "t": "Hazardous Materials"}, {"c": "FE-0127", "q": "You are treating a 45-year-old male with a suspected stroke. Upon assessment, you note facial droop, arm drift, and slurred speech. After performing a 12-lead ECG, what is your next priority?", "a": ["Transport the patient to the nearest stroke center", "Administer nitroglycerin 0.4 mg sublingually", "Provide oxygen via nasal cannula and wait for the ECG results", "Administer aspirin 324 mg orally"], "e": "In a patient exhibiting signs of a stroke—facial droop, arm drift, and slurred speech—the next priority is to transport the patient to the nearest stroke center as quickly as possible. Time is critical in stroke management because certain treatments, like thrombolytics, are time-sensitive and most effective when administered promptly. Rapid transport ensures that the patient receives advanced care without unnecessary delays.\n\nAdministering nitroglycerin is not indicated in stroke patients and may be harmful, especially if the stroke is hemorrhagic. Providing oxygen may be beneficial if the patient is hypoxic, but it should not delay transport. Administering aspirin is contraindicated in suspected stroke because it can worsen bleeding in a hemorrhagic stroke. Therefore, immediate transport to a specialized facility is the most appropriate action.", "s": "working", "t": "Neurology"}, {"c": "FE-0128", "q": "A 60-year-old male presents with sudden-onset abdominal pain that radiates to his back. He is diaphoretic, hypotensive with a blood pressure of 90/60 mmHg, and has a heart rate of 110 bpm. What condition should you suspect?", "a": ["Abdominal aortic aneurysm", "Pancreatitis", "Appendicitis", "Cholecystitis"], "e": "The patient is exhibiting classic signs of an abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA), specifically a ruptured AAA. Sudden-onset abdominal pain radiating to the back, diaphoresis, hypotension, and tachycardia are hallmark symptoms of this life-threatening condition. A ruptured AAA involves bleeding into the abdominal cavity due to a tear in the aortic wall, leading to shock from internal hemorrhage.\n\nPancreatitis can cause abdominal pain radiating to the back but usually presents with a history of alcohol use or gallstones and does not typically cause hypotension unless severe. Appendicitis causes right lower quadrant pain and does not radiate to the back. Cholecystitis involves gallbladder inflammation with pain in the right upper quadrant, often after fatty meals. None of these conditions align as closely with the patient's presentation as an AAA.", "s": "working", "t": "Abdominal and Gastrointestinal Disorders"}, {"c": "FE-0129", "q": "You are treating a 35-year-old male with severe difficulty breathing and angioedema after exposure to a known allergen. His breathing is labored, and he is unable to speak in full sentences. After administering albuterol, his condition does not improve. What is your next course of action?", "a": ["Administer epinephrine 0.3 mg intramuscularly", "Administer a second dose of albuterol", "Begin BVM ventilations with 100% oxygen", "Start CPAP at 5 cm H₂O pressure"], "e": "The patient is exhibiting signs of anaphylaxis, including severe respiratory distress and angioedema, which indicates airway swelling. Since albuterol has not improved his condition, the next critical step is to administer epinephrine 0.3 mg intramuscularly. Epinephrine is the first-line treatment for anaphylaxis as it rapidly reduces airway swelling, reverses bronchoconstriction, and improves cardiovascular stability.\n\nAdministering a second dose of albuterol is unlikely to alleviate angioedema because albuterol primarily targets bronchospasm, not systemic allergic reactions. Beginning BVM ventilations may be necessary if the patient's respiratory status deteriorates further, but addressing the underlying anaphylactic reaction with epinephrine is urgent. Starting CPAP may not be appropriate due to the risk of worsening airway obstruction in the presence of angioedema.", "s": "working", "t": "Immunology"}, {"c": "FE-0130", "q": "You are called to treat a 55-year-old female with a history of diabetes who is found unresponsive. Her blood glucose level is 35 mg/dL. After establishing IV access, what is the next appropriate intervention?", "a": ["Administer Dextrose (D50) 25 grams via IV push", "Administer 324 mg of aspirin orally", "Administer 0.3 mg of epinephrine intramuscularly", "Administer 1 mg of glucagon intramuscularly"], "e": "The patient is experiencing severe hypoglycemia with a blood glucose level of 35 mg/dL. After establishing IV access, the appropriate intervention is to administer 25 grams of dextrose intravenously. This rapidly increases blood glucose levels and restores cerebral function. Dextrose is the treatment of choice for hypoglycemia in patients with IV access because it acts quickly.\n\nAdministering aspirin is not indicated and could be harmful if the patient cannot swallow due to unresponsiveness. Epinephrine is not used to treat hypoglycemia and is inappropriate in this context. Glucagon intramuscularly is an alternative when IV access is unavailable; however, since IV access has been established, dextrose IV is preferred for its rapid effectiveness.", "s": "working", "t": "Endocrine Disorders"}, {"c": "FE-0131", "q": "You are treating a 65-year-old male with a suspected acute coronary syndrome (ACS). You have already administered aspirin. His blood pressure is 160/90, and he has no contraindications to nitroglycerin. What is your next step?", "a": ["Administer nitroglycerin 0.4 mg sublingually", "Administer epinephrine 0.3 mg intramuscularly", "Apply CPAP to improve oxygenation", "Transport immediately without further treatment"], "e": "In a patient with suspected ACS who has already received aspirin and has a blood pressure of 160/90 mmHg with no contraindications, the next appropriate step is to administer nitroglycerin 0.4 mg sublingually. Nitroglycerin helps to relieve chest pain by dilating the coronary arteries, reducing myocardial oxygen demand, and improving blood flow to the heart muscle.\n\nAdministering epinephrine is contraindicated in ACS as it can increase heart rate and myocardial oxygen consumption, potentially worsening the condition. Applying CPAP is not indicated unless the patient is experiencing pulmonary edema with hypoxia. Transporting without administering nitroglycerin misses the opportunity to provide symptomatic relief and could delay optimal patient care.", "s": "working", "t": "Cardiovascular"}];